window.WB_BOOK=window.WB_BOOK||{};window.WB_BOOK["week12"]={"id":"week12","n":13,"section":"E","label":"1-to-1 Session 5","title":"The Physics of the Atom","titleText":"The Physics of the Atom","subtitle":"Models of the atom, protons, neutrons and electrons, isotopes, alpha, beta and gamma radiation, nuclear equations, half-life, uses of radioisotopes and ⟪E=mc^{2}⟫","intro":"Sessions 1–3 covered all of Section D (electricity and magnetism). Today we move to Section E, the Physics of the Atom. This is the SHORTEST section in the syllabus and one of the best for picking up marks: most questions are \"state\", \"describe\" or \"explain\" questions with the same key words every year, plus three kinds of easy calculation (protons/neutrons, nuclear equations and half-life). There is also one formula, ⟪E=mc^{2}⟫. The topic builds in a straight line: (1) how scientists found out what is inside an atom, (2) what the atom is made of, (3) why some nuclei are unstable and give out radiation, (4) how fast that happens (half-life), and (5) how we use it and how dangerous it is. Paper 02 often has a whole question on this section, and Paper 01 always has several items. Learn the tables in this book until you can write them out from memory.","objectives":[{"code":"E1.1","text":"Describe the development of the model of the atom: Dalton, Thomson, Rutherford, Bohr and Chadwick"},{"code":"E1.2","text":"Describe the Geiger–Marsden (gold-foil) experiment and explain how it led to the nuclear model of the atom"},{"code":"E2.1","text":"Sketch simple atoms, showing the positions of protons, neutrons and electrons and the distribution of charge"},{"code":"E2.2","text":"Compare the mass and charge of the electron with those of the proton (and neutron)"},{"code":"E2.3","text":"Explain why atoms are electrically neutral and why they are stable"},{"code":"E2.4","text":"Define proton number Z, neutron number N and nucleon number A, and use ⟪A=Z+N⟫"},{"code":"E2.5","text":"Explain what isotopes are and give examples"},{"code":"E2.6","text":"Describe the shell model of the atom and relate it to the arrangement of the Periodic Table"},{"code":"E3.1","text":"Describe the contribution of Marie Curie to the study of radioactivity"},{"code":"E3.2","text":"State the nature of alpha (α), beta (β) and gamma (γ) radiation"},{"code":"E3.3","text":"Describe experiments to compare the ranges and penetrating power of α, β and γ radiation"},{"code":"E3.4","text":"Describe and interpret the tracks of α, β and γ radiation in a cloud chamber"},{"code":"E3.5","text":"Predict the deflection of α, β and γ radiation in magnetic and electric fields"},{"code":"E3.6","text":"Write and balance nuclear equations for α and β decay (conservation of A and Z)"},{"code":"E3.7","text":"Explain the random nature of radioactive decay, using a model such as throwing dice or coins"},{"code":"E3.8","text":"State that radioactive decay does not depend on physical or chemical conditions"},{"code":"E3.9","text":"Interpret decay graphs and show that the half-life is constant"},{"code":"E3.10","text":"Define half-life and solve problems involving half-life"},{"code":"E3.11","text":"Describe uses of radioisotopes in medicine, industry, agriculture and carbon dating, and the precautions needed"},{"code":"E3.12","text":"Use ⟪E=mc^{2}⟫ to relate the energy released in nuclear reactions to the loss of mass (mass defect)"},{"code":"E3.13","text":"Discuss the arguments for and against the use of nuclear energy"}],"formulaCard":[{"tex":"A=Z+N","meaning":"Nucleon number = proton number + neutron number; ⟪N=A-Z⟫","units":"no units (numbers of particles)"},{"tex":"{}^{A}_{Z}X","meaning":"Nuclide notation: A on top, Z at the bottom, X = symbol","units":""},{"tex":"\\text{Neutral atom: electrons }=\\text{ protons }=Z","meaning":"Why atoms have no overall charge","units":""},{"text":"Shells: 2, 8, 8 …","meaning":"Outer electrons = group number; number of shells = period","units":""},{"tex":"\\text{Proton: }+1,\\ \\text{ mass }1;\\quad \\text{ neutron: }0,\\ \\text{ mass }1;\\quad \\text{ electron: }-1,\\ \\text{ mass }1/1840","meaning":"Relative charges and masses","units":""},{"tex":"\\alpha ={}^{4}_{2}\\text{He};\\quad \\beta ={}^{0}_{-1}e;\\quad \\gamma =EM\\text{ wave}","meaning":"Nature of the three radiations","units":""},{"tex":"\\text{Stopped by: }\\alpha \\text{ paper};\\quad \\beta \\text{ few }\\mathrm{mm}Al;\\quad \\gamma \\text{ reduced by thick lead}","meaning":"Penetrating power (ionising: α > β > γ)","units":""},{"tex":"\\alpha \\text{ decay: }A-4,\\ Z-2","meaning":"⟪{}^{A}_{Z}X\\rightarrow {}^{A-4}_{Z-2}Y+{}^{4}_{2}⟫He","units":""},{"tex":"\\beta \\text{ decay: }A\\text{ same, }Z+1","meaning":"⟪{}^{A}_{Z}X\\rightarrow {}^{A}_{Z+1}Y+{}^{0}_{-1}e⟫ (neutron → proton + electron)","units":""},{"tex":"\\gamma \\text{ emission: no change in }A\\text{ or }Z","meaning":"Nucleus loses energy only","units":""},{"tex":"n=\\text{ total time }\\div T\\tfrac{1}{2}","meaning":"Number of half-lives","units":"T½ in s, min, h, days or years"},{"tex":"\\text{amount left }=\\text{ start }\\times (1/2)^{n}","meaning":"1 → 1/2 → 1/4 → 1/8 → 1/16 …","units":"same unit as the start (g, Bq, counts/min, nuclei)"},{"text":"corrected count rate = measured − background","meaning":"Remove background before finding half-life","units":"counts min^−1^ or counts s^−1^"},{"tex":"1\\,\\mathrm{Bq}=1\\text{ decay per second}","meaning":"Unit of activity","units":"Bq (becquerel)"},{"tex":"E=mc^{2}","meaning":"Energy from mass defect; ⟪c=3.0\\times 10^{8}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫, ⟪c^{2}=9.0\\times 10^{16}⟫","units":"E in J, m in kg"},{"tex":"\\text{Carbon-}14{:}\\ T\\tfrac{1}{2}=5730\\,\\mathrm{years}","meaning":"Carbon dating of once-living material","units":"years"}],"glossary":[{"term":"Nucleus","def":"Tiny, dense, positive centre of an atom containing protons and neutrons."},{"term":"Nucleon","def":"A proton or a neutron (a particle in the nucleus)."},{"term":"Proton number (Z)","def":"Number of protons in the nucleus; decides the element."},{"term":"Nucleon number (A)","def":"Total number of protons and neutrons in the nucleus."},{"term":"Isotopes","def":"Atoms of the same element with the same number of protons but different numbers of neutrons."},{"term":"Radioisotope","def":"An isotope with an unstable nucleus, which is radioactive."},{"term":"Radioactivity","def":"Spontaneous, random emission of α, β or γ radiation from unstable nuclei."},{"term":"Alpha particle","def":"Helium nucleus (2 protons + 2 neutrons) emitted from a nucleus."},{"term":"Beta particle","def":"Fast electron emitted from a nucleus when a neutron changes into a proton."},{"term":"Gamma ray","def":"High-energy electromagnetic wave emitted from a nucleus."},{"term":"Ionisation","def":"Removing or adding electrons to atoms, making ions."},{"term":"Background radiation","def":"Radiation always present in the environment from natural and artificial sources."},{"term":"Count rate","def":"Number of counts recorded by a detector per second or per minute."},{"term":"Activity","def":"Number of decays per second in a sample, measured in becquerels (Bq)."},{"term":"Half-life","def":"Time for half the radioactive nuclei in a sample to decay (activity to halve)."},{"term":"GM tube","def":"Geiger–Müller tube: a detector that produces a pulse (count) each time radiation enters it."},{"term":"Cloud chamber","def":"Chamber of supercooled vapour in which radiation leaves visible tracks of droplets."},{"term":"Mass defect","def":"Loss of mass in a nuclear reaction, converted into energy ⟪(E=mc^{2})⟫."},{"term":"Fission","def":"Splitting of a large nucleus into two smaller nuclei, releasing energy and neutrons."},{"term":"Fusion","def":"Joining of two light nuclei to form a heavier one, releasing energy (as in the Sun)."}],"lessons":[{"id":"week12.12.1","kind":"unit","unit":"12.1","title":"Models of the Atom and the Gold-Foil Experiment","titleText":"Models of the Atom and the Gold-Foil Experiment","goals":[{"code":"E1.1","text":"Describe the development of the model of the atom: Dalton, Thomson, Rutherford, Bohr and Chadwick"},{"code":"E1.2","text":"Describe the Geiger–Marsden (gold-foil) experiment and explain how it led to the nuclear model of the atom"}],"codes":["E1.1","E1.2"],"words":[{"term":"Nucleus","def":"Tiny, dense, positive centre of an atom containing protons and neutrons."},{"term":"Alpha particle","def":"Helium nucleus (2 protons + 2 neutrons) emitted from a nucleus."}],"learn":{"notes":[{"type":"p","text":"Nobody can see inside an atom. Scientists built a **model** (a picture that explains the results of experiments). Each time a new experiment gave a result the old model could not explain, the model was changed. CSEC expects you to know who did what, in order."},{"type":"table","headers":["Scientist (date)","What he found or suggested","His model of the atom"],"rows":[["**John Dalton** (about 1803)","Matter is made of tiny particles called atoms. Atoms of one element are all the same.","A tiny solid ball that cannot be split (indivisible)"],["**J. J. Thomson** (1897, model 1904)","Discovered the **electron**, a tiny negative particle that comes out of all atoms. So atoms CAN be split.","**\"Plum pudding\"**: a ball of positive charge with negative electrons dotted through it, like fruit in a pudding"],["**Ernest Rutherford** (1911), using the experiment of **Geiger and Marsden** (1909)","Fired α particles at thin gold foil. A few bounced back.","**Nuclear model**: a tiny, dense, positive **nucleus** in the centre, with electrons around it. The atom is mostly empty space."],["**Niels Bohr** (1913)","Electrons can only have certain fixed energies.","Electrons move in fixed orbits called **shells** (energy levels) around the nucleus"],["**James Chadwick** (1932)","Discovered the **neutron**, a neutral particle in the nucleus with about the same mass as a proton","Nucleus contains protons AND neutrons (this is the model we use today)"]]},{"type":"img","src":"img/week12/models","alt":"Figure 12.1 — Thomson's plum-pudding model (left) and the nuclear model of Rutherford and Bohr (right)","caption":"Figure 12.1 — Thomson's plum-pudding model (left) and the nuclear model of Rutherford and Bohr (right)","width":5.4,"w":1111,"h":577},{"type":"remember","text":"Memory aid for the order: **D**on't **T**ouch **R**adioactive **B**lack **C**ats → **D**alton, **T**homson, **R**utherford, **B**ohr, **C**hadwick."},{"type":"h","text":"The Geiger–Marsden (gold-foil) experiment"},{"type":"p","text":"In 1909 Hans Geiger and Ernest Marsden, working for Rutherford, fired a narrow beam of **alpha (α) particles** at a very thin sheet of **gold foil**. Alpha particles are small, fast and **positively charged**. A movable detector (a zinc sulfide screen that gives a tiny flash of light when hit, viewed through a microscope) counted the α particles that came out at each angle."},{"type":"img","src":"img/week12/gm_apparatus","alt":"Figure 12.2 — The Geiger–Marsden apparatus (seen from above)","caption":"Figure 12.2 — The Geiger–Marsden apparatus (seen from above)","width":5.6,"w":1208,"h":617},{"type":"bullets","items":["The apparatus is in a **vacuum**, so the α particles are not stopped by air (α particles travel only a few cm in air).","The gold foil is **very thin** (only a few hundred atoms thick), so each α particle passes close to at most one nucleus.","Gold is used because it can be hammered into very thin foil and has heavy nuclei with a large charge."]},{"type":"table","headers":["What they OBSERVED","What Rutherford CONCLUDED"],"rows":[["**Most** α particles went **straight through** the foil","Most of the atom is **empty space**"],["A **few** were **deflected** (bent) through small angles","There is a concentrated **positive** charge in the atom that repels the positive α particles that pass close to it"],["A **very few** (about 1 in 8000) **bounced back** (deflected by more than 90°)","The positive charge and almost all the mass are concentrated in a **very tiny** region: the **nucleus**. Only an α particle heading almost straight at it is turned back."]]},{"type":"img","src":"img/week12/alpha_paths","alt":"Figure 12.3 — Paths of α particles near one gold nucleus","caption":"Figure 12.3 — Paths of α particles near one gold nucleus","width":4.8,"w":984,"h":417},{"type":"def","term":"Nucleus","text":"the very small, dense, positively charged centre of an atom, containing protons and neutrons, where almost all of the mass of the atom is concentrated."},{"type":"p","text":"**Why the plum-pudding model failed:** in Thomson's model the positive charge is spread thinly over the whole atom. The force on an α particle would be weak, so ALL the α particles would go straight through or bend only slightly. NONE would bounce back. The bouncing back proved the positive charge must be concentrated in a tiny nucleus."},{"type":"remember","text":"Size: an atom is about 10^−10^ m across; the nucleus is about 10^−15^ m across, roughly 100 000 times smaller. If the atom were a cricket ground, the nucleus would be a pea in the middle."},{"type":"tip","text":"In \"explain\" questions, ALWAYS pair each observation with its conclusion (most through → empty space; few deflected → positive charge; very few back → tiny, dense nucleus). That is usually 1 mark for each observation and 1 for each conclusion."},{"type":"warn","text":"Saying the α particles \"hit\" the nucleus and bounce off like a ball off a wall. They never touch it. They are **repelled** by the positive charge of the nucleus (like charges repel)."}],"examples":[{"id":"week12.12.1.e1","title":"Observation → conclusion","question":"In the Geiger–Marsden experiment, state TWO observations and the conclusion Rutherford drew from each.","steps":[{"label":"Observation 1","text":"Most α particles passed straight through the gold foil."},{"label":"Conclusion 1","text":"The atom is mostly empty space."},{"label":"Observation 2","text":"A very small number bounced back towards the source."},{"label":"Conclusion 2","text":"The atom has a tiny, dense, positively charged nucleus that repels α particles heading straight at it."}],"answer":"Any two observation–conclusion pairs from the table (straight through → empty space; small deflection → positive charge; bounce back → tiny dense positive nucleus)."},{"id":"week12.12.1.e2","title":"Why the conditions matter","question":"Explain why (a) the experiment was done in a vacuum and (b) the gold foil was very thin.","steps":[{"label":"(a)","text":"α particles are absorbed by a few cm of air. In a vacuum there are no air molecules to stop or scatter them, so any deflection is caused by the gold."},{"label":"(b)","text":"With a very thin foil, each α particle passes near only one nucleus, so one deflection can be studied. It also means α particles are not absorbed by the foil."}],"answer":"(a) So the α particles are not absorbed/scattered by air. (b) So each α particle is scattered by at most one nucleus and is not absorbed."},{"id":"week12.12.1.e3","title":"Linking the scientists","question":"Thomson's model of the atom was replaced by Rutherford's. Rutherford's model was later improved by Bohr and Chadwick. State what each change added.","steps":[{"label":"Thomson → Rutherford","text":"the positive charge is not spread out; it is in a tiny central nucleus."},{"label":"Bohr","text":"electrons move in fixed shells (energy levels) around the nucleus."},{"label":"Chadwick","text":"the nucleus also contains neutral neutrons."}],"answer":"Rutherford: tiny positive nucleus, mostly empty space. Bohr: electrons in shells. Chadwick: neutrons in the nucleus."}]},"tryit":[{"id":"week12.12.1.p1","kind":"short","q":"Who discovered the electron?","marks":1,"level":1,"lines":1,"scheme":"J. J. Thomson (1).","points":[{"text":"J. J. Thomson","marks":1}],"accept":["J. J. Thomson","Thomson"],"section":"try"},{"id":"week12.12.1.p2","kind":"num","q":"Who discovered the neutron, and in roughly what year?","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":1,"scheme":"James Chadwick (1); 1932 (1).","points":[{"text":"James Chadwick","marks":1},{"text":"1932","marks":1}],"num":{"value":1932,"sf":4,"unit":"","unitTex":""},"section":"try"}],"practice":[{"id":"week12.12.1.p3","kind":"self","q":"Describe Thomson's \"plum pudding\" model of the atom. You may draw a labelled sketch.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":4,"scheme":"A sphere of positive charge (1) with negative electrons scattered/embedded through it (1).","points":[{"text":"A sphere of positive charge","marks":1},{"text":"with negative electrons scattered/embedded through it","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 3 (Linking the scientists). Start like this: Thomson → Rutherford: the positive charge is not spread out; it is in a tiny central nucleus.","from":3}},{"id":"week12.12.1.p4","kind":"self","q":"Put these models in the order they were proposed: nuclear model; solid indivisible sphere; electrons in shells; plum pudding.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"Solid sphere (Dalton) → plum pudding (Thomson) → nuclear model (Rutherford) → electrons in shells (Bohr). All correct (2); one pair swapped (1).","points":[{"text":"Solid sphere (Dalton) → plum pudding (Thomson) → nuclear model (Rutherford) → electrons in shells (Bohr). All correct","marks":2},{"text":"one pair swapped","marks":1}],"conf":"low","section":"practice"},{"id":"week12.12.1.p5","kind":"self","q":"In the gold-foil experiment, what type of particle was fired at the foil, and what charge does it have?","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":1,"scheme":"Alpha particles (1); positive (1).","points":[{"text":"Alpha particles","marks":1},{"text":"positive","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 2 (Why the conditions matter). Start like this: (a): α particles are absorbed by a few cm of air. In a vacuum there are no air molecules to stop or scatter them, so any deflection is caused by the gold.","from":2}},{"id":"week12.12.1.p6","kind":"self","q":"Explain why a few α particles were deflected through LARGE angles, using the idea of forces between charges.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":4,"scheme":"The nucleus is positive and the α particle is positive (1); like charges repel (1); an α particle passing very close to the nucleus feels a large repulsive force and is turned through a large angle (1).","points":[{"text":"The nucleus is positive and the α particle is positive","marks":1},{"text":"like charges repel","marks":1},{"text":"an α particle passing very close to the nucleus feels a large repulsive force and is turned through a large angle","marks":1}],"section":"practice"}],"exit":[{"id":"week12.12.1.p7","kind":"self","q":"Explain why the results of the Geiger–Marsden experiment could NOT be explained by the plum-pudding model.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":4,"scheme":"In the plum-pudding model the positive charge (and mass) is spread out over the whole atom (1); so the repulsive force on an α particle would be small (1); no α particles would be turned back, but some were (1).","points":[{"text":"In the plum-pudding model the positive charge (and mass) is spread out over the whole atom","marks":1},{"text":"so the repulsive force on an α particle would be small","marks":1},{"text":"no α particles would be turned back, but some were","marks":1}],"section":"exit"},{"id":"week12.12.1.p8","kind":"self","q":"The nucleus of an atom has a diameter of about ⟪1\\times 10^{-15}\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫ and the atom about ⟪1\\times 10^{-10}\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫. How many times bigger is the atom than the nucleus? What does this tell you about the atom?","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"⟪1\\times 10^{-10}\\div 1\\times 10^{-15}⟫ (1) ⟪=1\\times 10^{5}=100\\,000⟫ times (1); the atom is mostly empty space (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪1\\times 10^{-10}\\div 1\\times 10^{-15}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=1\\times 10^{5}=100\\,000⟫ times","marks":1},{"text":"the atom is mostly empty space","marks":1}],"section":"exit"}],"warmup":[{"id":"week12.12.1.w1","kind":"self","q":"State two ways to increase the e.m.f. induced when a magnet is pushed into a coil.","marks":1,"level":1,"scheme":"Any two: move faster; stronger magnet; more turns; larger area / iron core.","points":[{"text":"Any two: move faster; stronger magnet; more turns; larger area / iron core.","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week11.r1"},{"id":"week12.12.1.w2","kind":"self","q":"State Lenz's law.","marks":1,"level":1,"scheme":"The induced current opposes the change that produces it.","points":[{"text":"The induced current opposes the change that produces it.","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week11.r2"},{"id":"week12.12.1.w3","kind":"self","q":"Where is the coil when a generator's e.m.f. is zero?","marks":1,"level":1,"scheme":"Vertical — plane perpendicular to the field (sides move along the field lines).","points":[{"text":"Vertical — plane perpendicular to the field (sides move along the field lines).","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week11.r3"},{"id":"week12.12.1.w4","kind":"num","q":"⟪N_{p}=1000⟫, ⟪N_{s}=50⟫, ⟪V_{p}=240\\,\\mathrm{V}⟫. Find V~s~.","marks":1,"level":1,"scheme":"12 V.","points":[{"text":"12 V.","marks":1}],"num":{"value":12,"sf":2,"unit":"V","unitTex":"\\mathrm{V}"},"section":"warmup","src":"week11.r4"},{"id":"week12.12.1.w5","kind":"self","q":"Why is electricity transmitted at high voltage?","marks":1,"level":1,"scheme":"High voltage → small current ⟪(P=IV)⟫ → much less I²R heat loss in the cables.","points":[{"text":"High voltage → small current ⟪(P=IV)⟫ → much less I²R heat loss in the cables.","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week11.r5"}],"quickStart":[{"type":"p","text":"You met the atom in Session 1 (electrostatics). Check these facts before you start. Fill in each line, then check with the table in Unit 12.2."},{"type":"table","headers":["Idea you already know","What it says","Where you use it today"],"rows":[["Atoms contain charged particles","Protons (+) in the nucleus, electrons (−) outside it","Every unit in this book"],["Like charges repel, unlike attract","Law of electrostatics (Session 1)","Why α particles bounce off the + nucleus; deflection between charged plates"],["Force on a moving charge in a magnetic field","Fleming's left-hand rule (Session 3); current direction = direction of + charge","Why α and β bend in a magnetic field"],["Standard form","⟪3.0\\times 10^{8}⟫ means 3 with the point moved 8 places right","⟪E=mc^{2}⟫ calculations"]]},{"type":"fill","text":"Quick check 1: Which particle in an atom is negative? Where is it found?","lines":1},{"type":"fill","text":"Quick check 2: A positive and a negative charge are close together. Do they attract or repel?","lines":1},{"type":"fill","text":"Quick check 3: Write 300 000 000 in standard form. Then square it.","lines":1}],"n":93},{"id":"week12.12.2","kind":"unit","unit":"12.2","title":"Inside the Atom: Protons, Neutrons, Electrons and Isotopes","titleText":"Inside the Atom: Protons, Neutrons, Electrons and Isotopes","goals":[{"code":"E2.1","text":"Sketch simple atoms, showing the positions of protons, neutrons and electrons and the distribution of charge"},{"code":"E2.2","text":"Compare the mass and charge of the electron with those of the proton (and neutron)"},{"code":"E2.3","text":"Explain why atoms are electrically neutral and why they are stable"},{"code":"E2.4","text":"Define proton number Z, neutron number N and nucleon number A, and use ⟪A=Z+N⟫"},{"code":"E2.5","text":"Explain what isotopes are and give examples"},{"code":"E2.6","text":"Describe the shell model of the atom and relate it to the arrangement of the Periodic Table"}],"codes":["E2.1","E2.2","E2.3","E2.4","E2.5","E2.6"],"words":[{"term":"Nucleus","def":"Tiny, dense, positive centre of an atom containing protons and neutrons."},{"term":"Nucleon","def":"A proton or a neutron (a particle in the nucleus)."},{"term":"Proton number (Z)","def":"Number of protons in the nucleus; decides the element."},{"term":"Nucleon number (A)","def":"Total number of protons and neutrons in the nucleus."},{"term":"Isotopes","def":"Atoms of the same element with the same number of protons but different numbers of neutrons."},{"term":"Radioisotope","def":"An isotope with an unstable nucleus, which is radioactive."}],"learn":{"notes":[{"type":"p","text":"Today's model: every atom has a tiny **nucleus** made of **protons** and **neutrons** (together called **nucleons**). **Electrons** move around the nucleus in **shells**."},{"type":"table","headers":["Particle","Where it is","Relative charge","Relative mass","Actual mass / kg"],"rows":[["Proton","In the nucleus","+1","1","⟪1.67\\times 10^{-27}⟫"],["Neutron","In the nucleus","0 (neutral)","1","⟪1.67\\times 10^{-27}⟫ (very slightly more)"],["Electron","In shells around the nucleus","−1","1/1840 (almost zero)","⟪9.11\\times 10^{-31}⟫"]]},{"type":"p","text":"**Comparing the electron and the proton (E2.2):** they have charges that are **equal in size but opposite in sign** (each is ⟪1.6\\times 10^{-19}\\,\\mathrm{C}⟫). But the proton is about **1840 times heavier** than the electron. So almost all the mass of an atom is in the nucleus."},{"type":"h","text":"Sketching simple atoms"},{"type":"img","src":"img/week12/atoms","alt":"Figure 12.4 — Hydrogen, helium and lithium atoms (p = protons, n = neutrons; dots = electrons)","caption":"Figure 12.4 — Hydrogen, helium and lithium atoms (p = protons, n = neutrons; dots = electrons)","width":5.6,"w":1234,"h":466},{"type":"bullets","items":["Draw the nucleus as a small circle in the centre. Write the number of protons and neutrons inside it.","Draw each shell as a circle around it. Put the electrons on the shells as dots or crosses.","Charge distribution: ALL the positive charge is in the tiny nucleus; the negative charge (electrons) is spread out in the shells around it."]},{"type":"h","text":"Why atoms are neutral and stable (E2.3)"},{"type":"bullets","items":["**Neutral:** an atom has the **same number of electrons as protons**. Each proton has charge +1 and each electron −1, so the charges cancel. The overall charge is zero.","**Electrons stay in the atom** because the positive nucleus **attracts** the negative electrons (unlike charges attract).","**The nucleus stays together** even though the protons repel each other, because a very strong, short-range attractive force (the **strong nuclear force**) acts between all nucleons (protons and neutrons). The neutrons add to this \"glue\" without adding any repulsion."]},{"type":"remember","text":"If an atom gains or loses electrons it becomes an **ion** and is no longer neutral. Losing electrons → positive ion. Gaining electrons → negative ion. The number of PROTONS never changes in chemistry or charging."},{"type":"h","text":"Proton number, neutron number and nucleon number (E2.4)"},{"type":"def","term":"Proton number (atomic number), Z","text":"the number of protons in the nucleus of an atom. It decides which element the atom is."},{"type":"def","term":"Neutron number, N","text":"the number of neutrons in the nucleus of an atom."},{"type":"def","term":"Nucleon number (mass number), A","text":"the total number of protons and neutrons (nucleons) in the nucleus of an atom."},{"type":"formula","tex":"A=Z+N","where":["⟪A⟫ = nucleon (mass) number","⟪Z⟫ = proton (atomic) number","⟪N⟫ = neutron number, so ⟪N=A-Z⟫"],"label":"NUCLEON NUMBER"},{"type":"p","text":"We write a nuclide like this: **^A^~Z~X**, where X is the chemical symbol. Example: ^23^~11~Na means sodium with ⟪A=23⟫ and ⟪Z=11⟫. So it has 11 protons, ⟪23-11=12⟫ neutrons, and (as a neutral atom) 11 electrons."},{"type":"tip","text":"The BIG number on top is the BIGGER number (A, all the nucleons). The small number at the bottom is Z (protons). Neutrons are never written; you work them out: ⟪N=A-Z⟫."},{"type":"h","text":"Isotopes (E2.5)"},{"type":"def","term":"Isotopes","text":"atoms of the same element that have the same number of protons (same proton number Z) but different numbers of neutrons (different nucleon number A)."},{"type":"img","src":"img/week12/isotopes","alt":"Figure 12.5 — The three isotopes of hydrogen","caption":"Figure 12.5 — The three isotopes of hydrogen","width":5.6,"w":1188,"h":471},{"type":"table","headers":["Isotope","Symbol","Protons","Neutrons","Electrons","Note"],"rows":[["Carbon-12","^12^~6~C","6","6","6","Stable; about 99% of carbon"],["Carbon-14","^14^~6~C","6","8","6","Radioactive; used in carbon dating"],["Chlorine-35","^35^~17~Cl","17","18","17","About 75% of chlorine"],["Chlorine-37","^37^~17~Cl","17","20","17","About 25% of chlorine"],["Uranium-235","^235^~92~U","92","143","92","Fuel in nuclear power stations"],["Uranium-238","^238^~92~U","92","146","92","Most common uranium isotope"]]},{"type":"p","text":"Isotopes of an element have the **same chemical properties** (same number of electrons) but **different physical properties** such as mass and density. Some isotopes are stable; others are **radioactive** (called **radioisotopes**)."},{"type":"h","text":"The shell model and the Periodic Table (E2.6)"},{"type":"p","text":"Electrons fill the shells from the inside out. For the first 20 elements: the **1st shell holds up to 2** electrons, the **2nd up to 8**, the **3rd up to 8** (then the 4th starts)."},{"type":"table","headers":["Element","Z","Electron arrangement","Outer electrons → Group","Shells → Period"],"rows":[["Hydrogen","1","1","1 → (Group 1)","1"],["Helium","2","2","full shell → Group 0 (VIII)","1"],["Lithium","3","2,1","1 → Group I","2"],["Carbon","6","2,4","4 → Group IV","2"],["Neon","10","2,8","full shell → Group 0 (VIII)","2"],["Sodium","11","2,8,1","1 → Group I","3"],["Magnesium","12","2,8,2","2 → Group II","3"],["Chlorine","17","2,8,7","7 → Group VII","3"],["Argon","18","2,8,8","full shell → Group 0 (VIII)","3"],["Calcium","20","2,8,8,2","2 → Group II","4"]]},{"type":"bullets","items":["The **group** number = the number of electrons in the **outer shell**. Elements in the same group have similar chemical properties because they have the same number of outer electrons.","The **period** (row) number = the number of **occupied shells**.","Elements with a full outer shell (helium, neon, argon) are the unreactive noble gases (Group 0)."]},{"type":"warn","text":"Mixing up A and Z. Z (smaller) = protons. A (bigger) = protons + neutrons. The number of electrons in a NEUTRAL atom equals Z, not A."}],"examples":[{"id":"week12.12.2.e1","title":"Counting particles","question":"For the nuclide ^27^~13~Al, state the number of protons, neutrons and electrons in a neutral atom.","steps":[{"label":"Protons","text":"⟪Z=13⟫, so 13 protons"},{"label":"Formula","text":"⟪N=A-Z⟫"},{"label":"Substitute","text":"⟪N=27-13⟫"},{"label":"Answer","text":"⟪N=14⟫ neutrons"},{"label":"Electrons","text":"neutral, so electrons = protons ⟪=13⟫"}],"answer":"13 protons, 14 neutrons, 13 electrons."},{"id":"week12.12.2.e2","title":"Spotting isotopes","question":"Which two of these are isotopes of the same element? ^14^~6~X, ^14^~7~Y, ^12^~6~W, ^16^~8~V. Explain.","steps":[{"label":"Look at Z","text":"isotopes have the SAME bottom number. X and W both have ⟪Z=6⟫."},{"label":"Check A","text":"X has ⟪A=14⟫, W has ⟪A=12⟫, so they have different numbers of neutrons (8 and 6)."},{"label":"Note","text":"X and Y have the same A but different Z, so they are DIFFERENT elements."}],"answer":"X and W: same proton number (6) but different nucleon numbers (14 and 12), so different numbers of neutrons."},{"id":"week12.12.2.e3","title":"Shells and the Periodic Table","question":"Magnesium has proton number 12. Sketch the electron arrangement and state its group and period.","steps":[{"label":"Electrons","text":"neutral atom, so 12 electrons"},{"label":"Fill shells","text":"2 in the 1st shell, 8 in the 2nd, 2 left for the 3rd: 2,8,2"},{"label":"Group","text":"2 outer electrons → Group II"},{"label":"Period","text":"3 occupied shells → Period 3"}],"answer":"2,8,2; Group II; Period 3."},{"id":"week12.12.2.e4","title":"Comparing masses","question":"The mass of a proton is ⟪1.67\\times 10^{-27}\\,\\mathrm{kg}⟫ and the mass of an electron is ⟪9.11\\times 10^{-31}\\,\\mathrm{kg}⟫. How many times heavier is the proton?","steps":[{"label":"Method","text":"ratio = mass of proton ÷ mass of electron"},{"label":"Substitute","text":"ratio ⟪=1.67\\times 10^{-27}\\div 9.11\\times 10^{-31}⟫"},{"label":"Calculate","text":"ratio ⟪=1833\\approx 1800⟫"}],"answer":"About 1800 times heavier (1833). (The usual textbook figure is 1840; it depends on rounding.)"}]},"tryit":[{"id":"week12.12.2.p1","kind":"self","q":"State the relative charge and relative mass of (a) a proton, (b) a neutron, (c) an electron.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"(a) +1, 1 (1); (b) 0, 1 (1); (c) −1, ⟪1/1840\\ (\\approx 0)⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"(a) +1, 1","marks":1},{"text":"(b) 0, 1","marks":1},{"text":"(c) −1, ⟪1/1840\\ (\\approx 0)⟫","marks":1}],"section":"try","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 3 (Shells and the Periodic Table). Start like this: Electrons: neutral atom, so 12 electrons","from":3}},{"id":"week12.12.2.p2","kind":"self","q":"Define \"nucleon number\".","marks":1,"level":1,"lines":2,"scheme":"The total number of protons and neutrons in the nucleus of an atom (1).","points":[{"text":"The total number of protons and neutrons in the nucleus of an atom","marks":1}],"section":"try","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Counting particles). Start like this: Protons: ⟪Z=13⟫, so 13 protons","from":1}}],"practice":[{"id":"week12.12.2.p3","kind":"self","q":"An atom of potassium is written ^39^~19~K. How many protons, neutrons and electrons does a neutral atom contain?","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"19 protons (1); ⟪39-19=20⟫ neutrons (1); 19 electrons (1).","points":[{"text":"19 protons","marks":1},{"text":"⟪39-19=20⟫ neutrons","marks":1},{"text":"19 electrons","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Counting particles). Start like this: Protons: ⟪Z=13⟫, so 13 protons","from":1}},{"id":"week12.12.2.p4","kind":"self","q":"An atom has 26 protons and 30 neutrons. Write its nucleon number and proton number, and write it in the form ⟪{}^{A}_{Z}X\\ (X=Fe)⟫.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"⟪A=26+30=56⟫, ⟪Z=26⟫ (1); ^56^~26~Fe (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪A=26+30=56⟫, ⟪Z=26⟫","marks":1},{"text":"^56^~26~Fe","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Counting particles). Start like this: Protons: ⟪Z=13⟫, so 13 protons","from":1}},{"id":"week12.12.2.p5","kind":"self","q":"Explain why an atom has no overall charge.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"It has equal numbers of protons and electrons (1); the charge of each proton (+) is equal and opposite to the charge of each electron (−), so they cancel (1).","points":[{"text":"It has equal numbers of protons and electrons","marks":1},{"text":"the charge of each proton (+) is equal and opposite to the charge of each electron (−), so they cancel","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Counting particles). Start like this: Protons: ⟪Z=13⟫, so 13 protons","from":1}},{"id":"week12.12.2.p6","kind":"self","q":"Explain why the electrons do not fly away from the atom, and what holds the protons together in the nucleus.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Electrons are attracted to the positive nucleus (electrostatic attraction) (1); protons and neutrons are held together by the strong nuclear force, which is greater than the repulsion between protons (1).","points":[{"text":"Electrons are attracted to the positive nucleus (electrostatic attraction)","marks":1},{"text":"protons and neutrons are held together by the strong nuclear force, which is greater than the repulsion between protons","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Counting particles). Start like this: Protons: ⟪Z=13⟫, so 13 protons","from":1}},{"id":"week12.12.2.p7","kind":"self","q":"What are isotopes? Use ^35^~17~Cl and ^37^~17~Cl to explain your answer.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Atoms of the same element with the same number of protons but different numbers of neutrons (1); both have 17 protons (1); Cl-35 has 18 neutrons and Cl-37 has 20 neutrons (1).","points":[{"text":"Atoms of the same element with the same number of protons but different numbers of neutrons","marks":1},{"text":"both have 17 protons","marks":1},{"text":"Cl-35 has 18 neutrons and Cl-37 has 20 neutrons","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 2 (Spotting isotopes). Start like this: Look at Z: isotopes have the SAME bottom number. X and W both have ⟪Z=6⟫.","from":2}}],"exit":[{"id":"week12.12.2.p8","kind":"self","q":"Sketch a labelled diagram of an atom of carbon, ^12^~6~C, showing the protons, neutrons and electron shells.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":6,"scheme":"Nucleus labelled with 6 protons and 6 neutrons (1); two shells (1); 2 electrons in the 1st shell and 4 in the 2nd (1).","points":[{"text":"Nucleus labelled with 6 protons and 6 neutrons","marks":1},{"text":"two shells","marks":1},{"text":"2 electrons in the 1st shell and 4 in the 2nd","marks":1}],"section":"exit"},{"id":"week12.12.2.p9","kind":"self","q":"Chlorine ⟪(Z=17)⟫ and sodium ⟪(Z=11)⟫. Give the electron arrangement of each. Explain, using the shell model, which group of the Periodic Table each is in and why they are in the same period.","marks":4,"level":3,"lines":4,"scheme":"Cl: 2,8,7 (1); Na: 2,8,1 (1); Cl has 7 outer electrons → Group VII; Na has 1 → Group I (1); both have 3 occupied shells → both in Period 3 (1).","points":[{"text":"Cl: 2,8,7","marks":1},{"text":"Na: 2,8,1","marks":1},{"text":"Cl has 7 outer electrons → Group VII; Na has 1 → Group I","marks":1},{"text":"both have 3 occupied shells → both in Period 3","marks":1}],"section":"exit"}],"warmup":[{"id":"week12.12.2.w1","kind":"self","q":"Describe Thomson's \"plum pudding\" model of the atom. You may draw a labelled sketch.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":4,"scheme":"A sphere of positive charge (1) with negative electrons scattered/embedded through it (1).","points":[{"text":"A sphere of positive charge","marks":1},{"text":"with negative electrons scattered/embedded through it","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week12.12.1.p3"},{"id":"week12.12.2.w2","kind":"self","q":"Put these models in the order they were proposed: nuclear model; solid indivisible sphere; electrons in shells; plum pudding.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"Solid sphere (Dalton) → plum pudding (Thomson) → nuclear model (Rutherford) → electrons in shells (Bohr). All correct (2); one pair swapped (1).","points":[{"text":"Solid sphere (Dalton) → plum pudding (Thomson) → nuclear model (Rutherford) → electrons in shells (Bohr). All correct","marks":2},{"text":"one pair swapped","marks":1}],"conf":"low","section":"warmup","src":"week12.12.1.p4"},{"id":"week12.12.2.w3","kind":"num","q":"Who discovered the neutron, and in roughly what year?","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":1,"scheme":"James Chadwick (1); 1932 (1).","points":[{"text":"James Chadwick","marks":1},{"text":"1932","marks":1}],"num":{"value":1932,"sf":4,"unit":"","unitTex":""},"section":"warmup","src":"week12.12.1.p2"}],"n":94},{"id":"week12.12.3","kind":"unit","unit":"12.3","title":"Radioactivity: Alpha, Beta and Gamma Radiation","titleText":"Radioactivity: Alpha, Beta and Gamma Radiation","goals":[{"code":"E3.1","text":"Describe the contribution of Marie Curie to the study of radioactivity"},{"code":"E3.2","text":"State the nature of alpha (α), beta (β) and gamma (γ) radiation"},{"code":"E3.3","text":"Describe experiments to compare the ranges and penetrating power of α, β and γ radiation"},{"code":"E3.4","text":"Describe and interpret the tracks of α, β and γ radiation in a cloud chamber"},{"code":"E3.5","text":"Predict the deflection of α, β and γ radiation in magnetic and electric fields"}],"codes":["E3.1","E3.2","E3.3","E3.4","E3.5"],"words":[{"term":"Nucleus","def":"Tiny, dense, positive centre of an atom containing protons and neutrons."},{"term":"Radioactivity","def":"Spontaneous, random emission of α, β or γ radiation from unstable nuclei."},{"term":"Alpha particle","def":"Helium nucleus (2 protons + 2 neutrons) emitted from a nucleus."},{"term":"Beta particle","def":"Fast electron emitted from a nucleus when a neutron changes into a proton."},{"term":"Gamma ray","def":"High-energy electromagnetic wave emitted from a nucleus."},{"term":"Ionisation","def":"Removing or adding electrons to atoms, making ions."},{"term":"Background radiation","def":"Radiation always present in the environment from natural and artificial sources."},{"term":"Count rate","def":"Number of counts recorded by a detector per second or per minute."},{"term":"GM tube","def":"Geiger–Müller tube: a detector that produces a pulse (count) each time radiation enters it."},{"term":"Cloud chamber","def":"Chamber of supercooled vapour in which radiation leaves visible tracks of droplets."}],"learn":{"notes":[{"type":"p","text":"Some nuclei are **unstable** (they have the wrong balance of protons and neutrons). An unstable nucleus becomes more stable by throwing out a particle or a burst of energy. This is **radioactive decay**. It happens on its own: nothing has to start it."},{"type":"def","term":"Radioactivity","text":"the spontaneous and random emission of radiation (alpha particles, beta particles or gamma rays) from the nucleus of an unstable atom."},{"type":"h","text":"Marie Curie (E3.1)"},{"type":"bullets","items":["Polish-French scientist who worked in Paris with her husband **Pierre Curie**, after Henri Becquerel found that uranium gives out radiation (1896).","She invented the word **\"radioactivity\"** and showed that it comes from the **atoms themselves** (it does not depend on the chemical compound).","She discovered two new radioactive elements, **polonium** (named after Poland) and **radium** (1898).","She won **two Nobel Prizes** (Physics 1903, Chemistry 1911), the first person to do so.","Her work led to the use of radiation to treat cancer. She died from illness caused by long exposure to radiation, which showed its **dangers**."]},{"type":"h","text":"The three types of radiation (E3.2)"},{"type":"table","headers":["Property","Alpha (α)","Beta (β)","Gamma (γ)"],"rows":[["What it is","A **helium nucleus**: 2 protons + 2 neutrons (^4^~2~He)","A **fast electron** from the nucleus (^0^~−1~e)","An **electromagnetic wave** of very short wavelength (high energy)"],["Charge","+2","−1","0 (none)"],["Relative mass","4","1/1840 (very small)","0"],["Speed","Slow (about 1/20 of the speed of light)","Fast (up to 9/10 of the speed of light)","Speed of light, ⟪3.0\\times 10^{8}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫"],["Ionising power","**Strongest**","Medium","**Weakest**"],["Range in air","A few cm (about 5 cm)","About 1 m (tens of cm to a few m)","Very far (keeps getting weaker but never fully stopped)"],["Stopped by","A sheet of **paper** (or skin)","A few mm of **aluminium**","Reduced by several cm of **lead** or a metre of concrete"],["In a magnetic or electric field","Deflected a little (heavy)","Deflected a lot, the OPPOSITE way to α (light)","**Not deflected** (no charge)"]]},{"type":"def","term":"Ionisation","text":"the removal (or addition) of electrons from atoms, turning them into charged ions. Radiation causes ionisation as it passes through matter, and this is what damages living cells."},{"type":"remember","text":"The more strongly a radiation ionises, the faster it loses its energy, so the LESS it penetrates. α: most ionising, least penetrating. γ: least ionising, most penetrating."},{"type":"img","src":"img/week12/penetration","alt":"Figure 12.6 — Penetrating power of α, β and γ radiation","caption":"Figure 12.6 — Penetrating power of α, β and γ radiation","width":5.4,"w":974,"h":534},{"type":"h","text":"Experiment: comparing the penetrating power of α, β and γ (E3.3)"},{"type":"img","src":"img/week12/absorption_setup","alt":"Figure 12.7 — Apparatus for the absorption experiment","caption":"Figure 12.7 — Apparatus for the absorption experiment","width":5.2,"w":1174,"h":483},{"type":"p","text":"**Apparatus:** sealed radioactive source in a holder (handled with tongs), Geiger–Müller (GM) tube connected to a counter or ratemeter, sheets of paper, aluminium (1–5 mm) and lead (1–5 cm), metre rule, stopwatch."},{"type":"steps","items":["With NO source present, measure the count for several minutes and work out the **background count rate** (counts per minute). Background radiation comes from rocks, soil, building materials, cosmic rays from space, food and medical X-rays.","Place the source a fixed short distance (e.g. 2–3 cm) from the GM tube. Measure the count rate with nothing in between.","Place a sheet of paper between the source and the tube. Measure the count rate again.","Replace the paper with aluminium of increasing thickness, then with lead of increasing thickness, measuring the count rate each time.","Subtract the background count rate from every reading to get the **corrected** count rate.","(Range in air:) remove the absorbers and slowly move the GM tube away from the source, noting the distance at which the count rate falls to background."]},{"type":"table","headers":["Result","Conclusion"],"rows":[["Count rate falls sharply when paper is added","The source emits α"],["Count rate falls sharply when a few mm of aluminium is added","The source emits β"],["Count rate is still above background with aluminium; reduced only by thick lead","The source emits γ"],["Count rate falls to background a few cm from the source in air","α (range in air about 5 cm)"]]},{"type":"p","text":"**Precautions:** handle the source with long **tongs**, never with the hands; point it away from people; keep it in its **lead-lined box** when not in use; keep distance and time of exposure small; take several readings and average them (decay is random); keep the source-to-tube distance the same for every absorber."},{"type":"h","text":"Cloud-chamber tracks (E3.4)"},{"type":"p","text":"A **cloud chamber** contains air full of alcohol vapour, cooled (by dry ice) so it is ready to condense. Radiation passing through **ionises** the air. Droplets of liquid form on the ions, leaving a visible white trail, like the trail behind a jet plane."},{"type":"img","src":"img/week12/cloud_tracks","alt":"Figure 12.8 — Typical cloud-chamber tracks","caption":"Figure 12.8 — Typical cloud-chamber tracks","width":5.6,"w":1234,"h":454},{"type":"table","headers":["Radiation","Track","Why"],"rows":[["α","**Short, thick, straight**, all about the **same length**","Strongly ionising (many ions → thick); heavy, so not knocked off course; all α from one source have the same energy, so same range"],["β","**Thin, longer, wavy/twisted**, of different lengths","Weakly ionising (thin); very light, so easily knocked off course by collisions; β have a range of energies"],["γ","No clear track; only faint, short, scattered wisps","Very weakly ionising; it only shows where it knocks electrons out of atoms"]]},{"type":"h","text":"Deflection in magnetic and electric fields (E3.5)"},{"type":"img","src":"img/week12/deflect_magnetic","alt":"Figure 12.9 — α, β and γ in a magnetic field directed into the page","caption":"Figure 12.9 — α, β and γ in a magnetic field directed into the page","width":4.8,"w":1000,"h":514},{"type":"bullets","items":["A moving charge in a magnetic field feels a force (the motor effect). Use **Fleming's left-hand rule** for α (positive, so it acts like a current in its direction of motion).","β is negative, so it is deflected the **opposite way** to α.","β is deflected **much more** than α because it is about 7000 times lighter (even though its charge is only half as big).","γ has **no charge**, so it goes straight on."]},{"type":"img","src":"img/week12/deflect_electric","alt":"Figure 12.10 — α, β and γ passing between charged plates","caption":"Figure 12.10 — α, β and γ passing between charged plates","width":4.8,"w":1012,"h":532},{"type":"bullets","items":["α (+) is **attracted towards the negative plate**.","β (−) is **attracted towards the positive plate** and is bent more strongly.","γ is **not deflected**. (This is one way to prove γ rays carry no charge.)"]},{"type":"warn","text":"Saying gamma is \"stopped\" by lead. Lead only REDUCES (absorbs most of) gamma. Also, do not say α is deflected more than β: β bends MORE because it has a much smaller mass."},{"type":"tip","text":"In a magnetic field question, first find the direction for α with Fleming's left-hand rule (second finger = direction α moves). Then β goes the other way with a tighter curve, and γ goes straight."}],"examples":[{"id":"week12.12.3.e1","title":"Identifying radiation from absorption data","question":"A source is placed 3 cm from a GM tube. Background count rate ⟪=20⟫ counts/min. Readings: nothing between ⟪=500⟫ counts/min; paper ⟪=495⟫ counts/min; 3 mm aluminium ⟪=140⟫ counts/min; 2 cm lead ⟪=21⟫ counts/min. Which radiations does the source emit?","steps":[{"label":"Paper","text":"500 → 495 is almost no change (random variation), so there is NO α."},{"label":"Aluminium","text":"a large drop (495 → 140), so most of the radiation was β, stopped by aluminium."},{"label":"After aluminium","text":"140 is still far above background (20), so something passes through aluminium: γ."},{"label":"Lead","text":"21 ≈ background: the γ is absorbed by the lead."}],"answer":"β and γ (no α)."},{"id":"week12.12.3.e2","title":"Deflection reasoning","question":"A beam containing α, β and γ radiation passes between two parallel plates; the top plate is positive and the bottom negative. Describe the path of each radiation and explain.","steps":[{"label":"α","text":"positive, so attracted to the negative (bottom) plate; heavy, so it bends only a little."},{"label":"β","text":"negative, so attracted to the positive (top) plate; very light, so it bends a lot."},{"label":"γ","text":"no charge, so no force: it goes straight through."}],"answer":"α bends slightly downwards (towards −), β bends strongly upwards (towards +), γ is undeflected."},{"id":"week12.12.3.e3","title":"Cloud-chamber tracks","question":"A photograph of a cloud chamber shows several short, thick, straight tracks, all about 5 cm long, coming from one point. Identify the radiation and explain each feature.","steps":[{"label":"Identify","text":"α particles"},{"label":"Thick","text":"α is strongly ionising, producing many ions and so many droplets."},{"label":"Straight","text":"α is heavy, so collisions with air molecules do not knock it off course."},{"label":"Same length","text":"all α particles from the source have the same energy, so they travel the same distance (about 5 cm) before stopping."}],"answer":"Alpha radiation; thick = strongly ionising, straight = massive, same length = same energy."}]},"tryit":[{"id":"week12.12.3.p1","kind":"self","q":"State the nature of (a) an α particle, (b) a β particle, (c) γ radiation.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"(a) Helium nucleus / 2 protons + 2 neutrons (1); (b) fast-moving electron (from the nucleus) (1); (c) electromagnetic wave/radiation of very short wavelength (1).","points":[{"text":"(a) Helium nucleus / 2 protons + 2 neutrons","marks":1},{"text":"(b) fast-moving electron (from the nucleus)","marks":1},{"text":"(c) electromagnetic wave/radiation of very short wavelength","marks":1}],"section":"try","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Identifying radiation from absorption data). Start like this: Paper: 500 → 495 is almost no change (random variation), so there is NO α.","from":1}},{"id":"week12.12.3.p2","kind":"self","q":"Which radiation is (a) the most ionising, (b) the most penetrating, (c) stopped by a sheet of paper?","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"(a) α (1); (b) γ (1); (c) α (1).","points":[{"text":"(a) α","marks":1},{"text":"(b) γ","marks":1},{"text":"(c) α","marks":1}],"section":"try","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Identifying radiation from absorption data). Start like this: Paper: 500 → 495 is almost no change (random variation), so there is NO α.","from":1}}],"practice":[{"id":"week12.12.3.p3","kind":"self","q":"State THREE contributions Marie Curie made to the study of radioactivity.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":4,"scheme":"Any three: introduced the term radioactivity (1); showed it is a property of the atom/nucleus, not the chemical compound (1); discovered polonium (1); discovered radium (1); won two Nobel Prizes / pioneered use of radiation in medicine (1).","points":[{"text":"Any three: introduced the term radioactivity","marks":1},{"text":"showed it is a property of the atom/nucleus, not the chemical compound","marks":1},{"text":"discovered polonium","marks":1},{"text":"discovered radium","marks":1},{"text":"won two Nobel Prizes / pioneered use of radiation in medicine","marks":1}],"conf":"low","section":"practice"},{"id":"week12.12.3.p4","kind":"self","q":"What is background radiation? Give TWO sources.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Radiation that is always present in the environment (from natural and artificial sources) (1); any two: rocks/soil (radon gas), cosmic rays, building materials, food/drink, medical X-rays, nuclear fallout (1)(1).","points":[{"text":"Radiation that is always present in the environment (from natural and artificial sources)","marks":1},{"text":"any two: rocks/soil (radon gas), cosmic rays, building materials, food/drink, medical X-rays, nuclear fallout","marks":2}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Identifying radiation from absorption data). Start like this: Paper: 500 → 495 is almost no change (random variation), so there is NO α.","from":1}},{"id":"week12.12.3.p5","kind":"self","q":"In the absorption experiment, why must the background count rate be measured first?","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"So it can be subtracted from each reading (1), leaving only the count rate due to the source (corrected count rate) (1).","points":[{"text":"So it can be subtracted from each reading","marks":1},{"text":"leaving only the count rate due to the source (corrected count rate)","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Identifying radiation from absorption data). Start like this: Paper: 500 → 495 is almost no change (random variation), so there is NO α.","from":1}},{"id":"week12.12.3.p6","kind":"self","q":"State TWO safety precautions when using a radioactive source in the laboratory.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"Any two: handle with tongs; keep at arm's length / point away from body; store in a lead-lined box; minimise time of use; wash hands; never eat or drink in the lab (1)(1).","points":[{"text":"Any two: handle with tongs; keep at arm's length / point away from body; store in a lead-lined box; minimise time of use; wash hands; never eat or drink in the lab","marks":2}],"section":"practice"},{"id":"week12.12.3.p7","kind":"self","q":"Describe the cloud-chamber track of a β particle and explain why it looks like that.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Thin, long and wavy/twisted (1); thin because β is weakly ionising (1); wavy because β is very light and is easily deflected by collisions with air molecules (1).","points":[{"text":"Thin, long and wavy/twisted","marks":1},{"text":"thin because β is weakly ionising","marks":1},{"text":"wavy because β is very light and is easily deflected by collisions with air molecules","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 3 (Cloud-chamber tracks). Start like this: Identify: α particles","from":3}}],"exit":[{"id":"week12.12.3.p8","kind":"self","q":"A beam of α, β and γ radiation enters a magnetic field directed INTO the page, moving from left to right. Using Fleming's left-hand rule, the force on α is towards the top of the page. Describe the paths of α, β and γ, and explain why β is deflected more than α.","marks":4,"level":3,"lines":4,"scheme":"α curves gently upwards (1); β curves downwards (opposite way, as it is negative) (1); γ goes straight on (no charge) (1); β is deflected more because its mass is much smaller (about 1/7000 of α) (1).","points":[{"text":"α curves gently upwards","marks":1},{"text":"β curves downwards (opposite way, as it is negative)","marks":1},{"text":"γ goes straight on (no charge)","marks":1},{"text":"β is deflected more because its mass is much smaller (about 1/7000 of α)","marks":1}],"section":"exit"},{"id":"week12.12.3.p9","kind":"self","q":"A source gives these corrected count rates: no absorber 300 counts/min; paper 160 counts/min; 3 mm aluminium 158 counts/min; 3 cm lead 4 counts/min. Which radiations does it emit? Explain.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":4,"scheme":"α: count rate falls sharply with paper (300 → 160) (1); no β: aluminium makes almost no further difference (160 → 158) (1); γ: radiation passes through aluminium and is only reduced by lead (1).","points":[{"text":"α: count rate falls sharply with paper (300 → 160)","marks":1},{"text":"no β: aluminium makes almost no further difference (160 → 158)","marks":1},{"text":"γ: radiation passes through aluminium and is only reduced by lead","marks":1}],"section":"exit"}],"warmup":[{"id":"week12.12.3.w1","kind":"self","q":"An atom has 26 protons and 30 neutrons. Write its nucleon number and proton number, and write it in the form ⟪{}^{A}_{Z}X\\ (X=Fe)⟫.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"⟪A=26+30=56⟫, ⟪Z=26⟫ (1); ^56^~26~Fe (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪A=26+30=56⟫, ⟪Z=26⟫","marks":1},{"text":"^56^~26~Fe","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week12.12.2.p4"},{"id":"week12.12.3.w2","kind":"self","q":"Explain why an atom has no overall charge.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"It has equal numbers of protons and electrons (1); the charge of each proton (+) is equal and opposite to the charge of each electron (−), so they cancel (1).","points":[{"text":"It has equal numbers of protons and electrons","marks":1},{"text":"the charge of each proton (+) is equal and opposite to the charge of each electron (−), so they cancel","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week12.12.2.p5"},{"id":"week12.12.3.w3","kind":"self","q":"Explain why the electrons do not fly away from the atom, and what holds the protons together in the nucleus.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Electrons are attracted to the positive nucleus (electrostatic attraction) (1); protons and neutrons are held together by the strong nuclear force, which is greater than the repulsion between protons (1).","points":[{"text":"Electrons are attracted to the positive nucleus (electrostatic attraction)","marks":1},{"text":"protons and neutrons are held together by the strong nuclear force, which is greater than the repulsion between protons","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week12.12.2.p6"}],"n":95},{"id":"week12.12.4","kind":"unit","unit":"12.4","title":"Nuclear Equations","titleText":"Nuclear Equations","goals":[{"code":"E3.6","text":"Write and balance nuclear equations for α and β decay (conservation of A and Z)"}],"codes":["E3.6"],"words":[{"term":"Nucleus","def":"Tiny, dense, positive centre of an atom containing protons and neutrons."},{"term":"Nucleon","def":"A proton or a neutron (a particle in the nucleus)."},{"term":"Nucleon number (A)","def":"Total number of protons and neutrons in the nucleus."},{"term":"Alpha particle","def":"Helium nucleus (2 protons + 2 neutrons) emitted from a nucleus."},{"term":"Beta particle","def":"Fast electron emitted from a nucleus when a neutron changes into a proton."},{"term":"Gamma ray","def":"High-energy electromagnetic wave emitted from a nucleus."}],"learn":{"notes":[{"type":"p","text":"When a nucleus decays it turns into the nucleus of a **different element** (because its number of protons changes). The original nucleus is called the **parent**; the new one is the **daughter**. We show this with a **nuclear equation**."},{"type":"table","headers":["Particle","Symbol in equations","A (top)","Z (bottom)"],"rows":[["Alpha particle","^4^~2~He (or ^4^~2~α)","4","2"],["Beta particle (electron)","^0^~−1~e (or ^0^~−1~β)","0","−1"],["Gamma ray","γ","0","0"],["Proton","^1^~1~p","1","1"],["Neutron","^1^~0~n","1","0"]]},{"type":"remember","text":"**The golden rule:** in a nuclear equation the **top numbers (A) add up to the same total on both sides**, and the **bottom numbers (Z) add up to the same total on both sides**. Nucleons and charge are conserved."},{"type":"h","text":"Alpha decay"},{"type":"p","text":"The nucleus loses 2 protons and 2 neutrons. So **A goes down by 4** and **Z goes down by 2**. The element moves 2 places back in the Periodic Table."},{"type":"formula","tex":"{}^{A}_{Z}X\\rightarrow {}^{A-4}_{Z-2}Y+{}^{4}_{2}\\text{He}","where":["Example: ⟪{}^{226}_{88}Ra\\rightarrow {}^{222}_{86}Rn+{}^{4}_{2}⟫He  (radium → radon)","Check top: ⟪226=222+4⟫ ✓   Check bottom: ⟪88=86+2⟫ ✓"],"label":"ALPHA DECAY"},{"type":"h","text":"Beta decay"},{"type":"p","text":"Inside the nucleus a **neutron changes into a proton and an electron**: ⟪{}^{1}_{0}n\\rightarrow {}^{1}_{1}p+{}^{0}_{-1}e⟫. The electron is shot out as the β particle; the proton stays. So **A stays the same** and **Z goes UP by 1**. The element moves 1 place forward in the Periodic Table."},{"type":"formula","tex":"{}^{A}_{Z}X\\rightarrow {}^{A}_{Z+1}Y+{}^{0}_{-1}e","where":["Example: ⟪{}^{14}_{6}C\\rightarrow {}^{14}_{7}N+{}^{0}_{-1}e⟫  (carbon → nitrogen)","Check top: ⟪14=14+0⟫ ✓   Check bottom: ⟪6=7+(-1)⟫ ✓"],"label":"BETA DECAY"},{"type":"h","text":"Gamma emission"},{"type":"p","text":"Gamma is pure energy. After α or β decay the new nucleus often has extra energy and gives it out as a γ ray. **A and Z do not change**, so the element stays the same: ^60^~28~Ni (excited, with extra energy) → ⟪{}^{60}_{28}Ni+\\gamma⟫."},{"type":"table","headers":["Type of decay","Change in A","Change in Z","Change in N","Element"],"rows":[["α","−4","−2","−2","Changes (2 places back)"],["β","0","+1","−1","Changes (1 place forward)"],["γ","0","0","0","No change"]]},{"type":"h","text":"Method: finding a missing nucleus"},{"type":"steps","items":["Write the equation with the unknown as ^A^~Z~X.","Top numbers: (left total) ⟪=A⟫ + (top numbers of everything else on the right). Solve for A.","Bottom numbers: (left total) ⟪=Z⟫ + (bottom numbers of everything else on the right). Solve for Z.","Use Z to name the element (from the Periodic Table or the data given). Z decides the element."]},{"type":"warn","text":"Writing the β particle as ^0^~1~e or ^1^~0~e. It is ^0^~−1~e: mass number 0, charge −1. And in β decay Z goes UP (not down) because a neutron becomes a proton."},{"type":"tip","text":"Always write the two checks (top total and bottom total) beside your equation. Examiners give a mark for a correctly balanced equation, and the check catches nearly every mistake."}],"examples":[{"id":"week12.12.4.e1","title":"Alpha decay of uranium","question":"Uranium-238 (^238^~92~U) decays by emitting an α particle to form thorium (Th). Write the nuclear equation.","steps":[{"label":"Set up","text":"⟪{}^{238}_{92}U\\rightarrow {}^{A}_{Z}Th+{}^{4}_{2}⟫He"},{"label":"Top","text":"⟪238=A+4⟫, so ⟪A=234⟫"},{"label":"Bottom","text":"⟪92=Z+2⟫, so ⟪Z=90⟫"}],"answer":"⟪{}^{238}_{92}U\\rightarrow {}^{234}_{90}Th+{}^{4}_{2}⟫He"},{"id":"week12.12.4.e2","title":"Beta decay of strontium","question":"Strontium-90 (^90^~38~Sr) is a β emitter. It decays to yttrium (Y). Write the equation.","steps":[{"label":"Set up","text":"⟪{}^{90}_{38}Sr\\rightarrow {}^{A}_{Z}Y+{}^{0}_{-1}e⟫"},{"label":"Top","text":"⟪90=A+0⟫, so ⟪A=90⟫"},{"label":"Bottom","text":"⟪38=Z+(-1)⟫, so ⟪Z=39⟫"}],"answer":"⟪{}^{90}_{38}Sr\\rightarrow {}^{90}_{39}Y+{}^{0}_{-1}e⟫"},{"id":"week12.12.4.e3","title":"A decay chain","question":"^238^~92~U decays by α emission, then the product decays by β emission, and that product decays by β emission again. Find A and Z of the final nucleus. Which element is it?","steps":[{"label":"After α","text":"⟪A=238-4=234⟫, ⟪Z=92-2=90⟫"},{"label":"After 1st β","text":"⟪A=234⟫, ⟪Z=90+1=91⟫"},{"label":"After 2nd β","text":"⟪A=234⟫, ⟪Z=91+1=92⟫"},{"label":"Element","text":"⟪Z=92⟫ is uranium again"}],"answer":"^234^~92~U: an isotope of uranium (same Z as the start, 4 fewer neutrons)."},{"id":"week12.12.4.e4","title":"How many α and β?","question":"Thorium-232 (^232^~90~Th) decays through a series of α and β emissions to the stable lead-208 (^208^~82~Pb). How many α and how many β particles are emitted?","steps":[{"label":"α from A","text":"only α changes A. Drop in ⟪A=232-208=24⟫. Number of ⟪\\alpha =24\\div 4=6⟫"},{"label":"Z after 6 α","text":"⟪Z=90-(6\\times 2)=78⟫"},{"label":"β from Z","text":"final Z is 82, so Z must go UP by ⟪82-78=4⟫. Each β raises Z by 1, so 4 β"}],"answer":"6 α particles and 4 β particles."}]},"tryit":[{"id":"week12.12.4.p1","kind":"self","q":"State what happens to (a) the nucleon number and (b) the proton number of a nucleus when it emits an α particle.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"(a) Decreases by 4 (1); (b) decreases by 2 (1).","points":[{"text":"(a) Decreases by 4","marks":1},{"text":"(b) decreases by 2","marks":1}],"section":"try"},{"id":"week12.12.4.p2","kind":"self","q":"Explain why the proton number INCREASES by 1 in β decay.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"A neutron in the nucleus changes into a proton and an electron (1); the electron is emitted and the proton stays, so there is one more proton (1).","points":[{"text":"A neutron in the nucleus changes into a proton and an electron","marks":1},{"text":"the electron is emitted and the proton stays, so there is one more proton","marks":1}],"section":"try"}],"practice":[{"id":"week12.12.4.p3","kind":"self","q":"Why does γ emission NOT change the element?","marks":1,"level":1,"lines":2,"scheme":"γ has no mass and no charge, so A and Z (number of protons) are unchanged (1).","points":[{"text":"γ has no mass and no charge, so A and Z (number of protons) are unchanged","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 3 (A decay chain). Start like this: After α: ⟪A=238-4=234⟫, ⟪Z=92-2=90⟫","from":3}},{"id":"week12.12.4.p4","kind":"self","q":"Complete: ⟪{}^{210}_{84}Po\\rightarrow {}^{?}_{?}Pb+{}^{4}_{2}⟫He","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":1,"scheme":"⟪A=210-4=206⟫ (1); ⟪Z=84-2=82\\ (1)⟫: ^206^~82~Pb.","points":[{"text":"⟪A=210-4=206⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪Z=84-2=82⟫. : ^206^~82~Pb","marks":1}],"section":"practice"},{"id":"week12.12.4.p5","kind":"self","q":"Complete: ⟪{}^{131}_{53}I\\rightarrow {}^{?}_{?}Xe+{}^{0}_{-1}e⟫","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":1,"scheme":"⟪A=131⟫ (1); ⟪Z=53+1=54\\ (1)⟫: ^131^~54~Xe.","points":[{"text":"⟪A=131⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪Z=53+1=54⟫. : ^131^~54~Xe","marks":1}],"section":"practice"},{"id":"week12.12.4.p6","kind":"self","q":"Radium-226 (^226^~88~Ra) decays to radon-222 (^222^~86~Rn). What particle is emitted? Write the full equation.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"A falls by 4 and Z by 2, so an α particle (1); ⟪{}^{226}_{88}Ra\\rightarrow {}^{222}_{86}Rn+{}^{4}_{2}⟫He (2: correct products 1, balanced 1).","points":[{"text":"A falls by 4 and Z by 2, so an α particle","marks":1},{"text":"⟪{}^{226}_{88}Ra\\rightarrow {}^{222}_{86}Rn+{}^{4}_{2}⟫He","marks":2}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Alpha decay of uranium). Start like this: Set up: ⟪{}^{238}_{92}U\\rightarrow {}^{A}_{Z}Th+{}^{4}_{2}⟫He","from":1}},{"id":"week12.12.4.p7","kind":"self","q":"In the equation ⟪{}^{214}_{82}Pb\\rightarrow {}^{214}_{83}Bi+X⟫, identify X and explain how you know.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"⟪X=\\beta⟫ particle, ^0^~−1~e (1); A unchanged and Z increased by 1 (top: ⟪214=214+0⟫; bottom: ⟪82=83+(-1)⟫) (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪X=\\beta⟫ particle, ^0^~−1~e","marks":1},{"text":"A unchanged and Z increased by 1 (top: ⟪214=214+0⟫; bottom: ⟪82=83+(-1)⟫)","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Alpha decay of uranium). Start like this: Set up: ⟪{}^{238}_{92}U\\rightarrow {}^{A}_{Z}Th+{}^{4}_{2}⟫He","from":1}}],"exit":[{"id":"week12.12.4.p8","kind":"self","q":"^234^~90~Th emits a β particle, and the product then emits an α particle. Find the nucleon number and proton number of the final nucleus.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"After β: ⟪A=234⟫, ⟪Z=91⟫ (1); after α: ⟪A=230⟫ (1), ⟪Z=89⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"After β: ⟪A=234⟫, ⟪Z=91⟫","marks":1},{"text":"after α: ⟪A=230⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪Z=89⟫","marks":1}],"section":"exit"},{"id":"week12.12.4.p9","kind":"self","q":"Uranium-235 (^235^~92~U) decays to lead-207 (^207^~82~Pb) by a series of α and β emissions. How many of each are emitted? Show your working.","marks":4,"level":3,"lines":4,"scheme":"Drop in ⟪A=235-207=28⟫ (1); number of ⟪\\alpha =28\\div 4=7⟫ (1); Z after ⟪7\\alpha =92-14=78⟫ (1); ⟪82-78=4⟫, so 4 β (1).","points":[{"text":"Drop in ⟪A=235-207=28⟫","marks":1},{"text":"number of ⟪\\alpha =28\\div 4=7⟫","marks":1},{"text":"Z after ⟪7\\alpha =92-14=78⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪82-78=4⟫, so 4 β","marks":1}],"section":"exit"}],"warmup":[{"id":"week12.12.4.w1","kind":"self","q":"Which radiation is (a) the most ionising, (b) the most penetrating, (c) stopped by a sheet of paper?","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"(a) α (1); (b) γ (1); (c) α (1).","points":[{"text":"(a) α","marks":1},{"text":"(b) γ","marks":1},{"text":"(c) α","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week12.12.3.p2"},{"id":"week12.12.4.w2","kind":"self","q":"In the absorption experiment, why must the background count rate be measured first?","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"So it can be subtracted from each reading (1), leaving only the count rate due to the source (corrected count rate) (1).","points":[{"text":"So it can be subtracted from each reading","marks":1},{"text":"leaving only the count rate due to the source (corrected count rate)","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week12.12.3.p5"},{"id":"week12.12.4.w3","kind":"self","q":"State TWO safety precautions when using a radioactive source in the laboratory.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"Any two: handle with tongs; keep at arm's length / point away from body; store in a lead-lined box; minimise time of use; wash hands; never eat or drink in the lab (1)(1).","points":[{"text":"Any two: handle with tongs; keep at arm's length / point away from body; store in a lead-lined box; minimise time of use; wash hands; never eat or drink in the lab","marks":2}],"section":"warmup","src":"week12.12.3.p6"}],"n":96},{"id":"week12.12.5","kind":"unit","unit":"12.5","title":"Random Decay and Half-Life","titleText":"Random Decay and Half-Life","goals":[{"code":"E3.7","text":"Explain the random nature of radioactive decay, using a model such as throwing dice or coins"},{"code":"E3.8","text":"State that radioactive decay does not depend on physical or chemical conditions"},{"code":"E3.9","text":"Interpret decay graphs and show that the half-life is constant"},{"code":"E3.10","text":"Define half-life and solve problems involving half-life"}],"codes":["E3.7","E3.8","E3.9","E3.10"],"words":[{"term":"Nucleus","def":"Tiny, dense, positive centre of an atom containing protons and neutrons."},{"term":"Radioisotope","def":"An isotope with an unstable nucleus, which is radioactive."},{"term":"Background radiation","def":"Radiation always present in the environment from natural and artificial sources."},{"term":"Count rate","def":"Number of counts recorded by a detector per second or per minute."},{"term":"Activity","def":"Number of decays per second in a sample, measured in becquerels (Bq)."},{"term":"Half-life","def":"Time for half the radioactive nuclei in a sample to decay (activity to halve)."},{"term":"GM tube","def":"Geiger–Müller tube: a detector that produces a pulse (count) each time radiation enters it."}],"learn":{"notes":[{"type":"h","text":"Decay is random (E3.7)"},{"type":"p","text":"A radioactive sample contains billions of unstable nuclei. We can NEVER say **which** nucleus will decay next, or **when** a particular nucleus will decay. Each nucleus has the same chance of decaying in the next second. This is what **random** means. What we CAN predict is what fraction of a LARGE number of nuclei will decay in a given time."},{"type":"p","text":"Evidence for randomness: a GM tube near a source does not click at a steady rate. The count in each 10-second period jumps up and down (for example 52, 47, 58, 44, 51). That is why we take several readings and average them."},{"type":"h","text":"The dice model of decay"},{"type":"steps","items":["Take a large number of dice (e.g. 120). Each die stands for one unstable nucleus.","Throw them all. Every die that shows a **six** has \"decayed\". Remove those dice and count how many are left.","Throw the remaining dice again and remove the sixes. Repeat for 8–10 throws.","Plot a graph of the number of dice left against the throw number."]},{"type":"img","src":"img/week12/dice_model","alt":"Figure 12.11 — Typical results of the dice model (curve = expected values)","caption":"Figure 12.11 — Typical results of the dice model (curve = expected values)","width":4.4,"w":1014,"h":574},{"type":"bullets","items":["You cannot predict WHICH die will show a six: like a nucleus, each one is random.","But about **1/6** of the dice left are removed each throw, so the number left falls by the same FRACTION each time. The graph has the same shape as a radioactive decay curve.","It takes about the same number of throws (about 4) to halve the number of dice, whatever number you start with: a constant \"half-life\".","Coins work too: remove every coin that lands \"heads\". Then half are removed each throw, so the \"half-life\" is exactly 1 throw."]},{"type":"h","text":"Decay does not depend on conditions (E3.8)"},{"type":"p","text":"The rate of radioactive decay is **not affected** by temperature, pressure, light, or whether the atom is in a chemical compound or not. Decay happens in the nucleus; heating or chemical reactions only affect the electrons. So the half-life of an isotope is a **fixed** property of that isotope."},{"type":"h","text":"Activity, count rate and half-life (E3.9, E3.10)"},{"type":"def","term":"Activity","text":"the number of nuclei in a sample that decay per second. It is measured in **becquerels (Bq)**: ⟪1\\,\\mathrm{Bq}=1⟫ decay per second. In the lab we measure the **count rate** (counts per minute or per second) with a GM tube."},{"type":"def","term":"Half-life (T½)","text":"the time taken for half of the radioactive nuclei in a sample to decay, OR the time taken for the activity (count rate) of a sample to fall to half of its original value."},{"type":"img","src":"img/week12/decay_curve","alt":"Figure 12.12 — A decay curve. The count rate halves every 2 minutes.","caption":"Figure 12.12 — A decay curve. The count rate halves every 2 minutes.","width":5,"w":1094,"h":694},{"type":"p","text":"**Showing the half-life is constant:** on Figure 12.12, 800 → 400 takes 2 minutes; 400 → 200 takes another 2 minutes; 200 → 100 takes another 2 minutes. Starting from ANY point, the time to halve is always the same, 2 minutes. That is how you show from a graph that the half-life is constant."},{"type":"steps","items":["Read the starting count rate on the y-axis (e.g. 800).","Halve it (400). Draw a line across from 400 to the curve, then down to the time axis. Read the time.","Repeat from 400 → 200 (and 200 → 100). The time intervals should be equal.","Give the half-life as the average of your readings."]},{"type":"h","text":"Half-life calculations"},{"type":"p","text":"**Method 1 – halving table (use this, it is the safest):** write the time and the amount, and keep halving."},{"type":"table","headers":["Number of half-lives","0","1","2","3","4","5"],"rows":[["Fraction left","1","1/2","1/4","1/8","1/16","1/32"],["Fraction decayed","0","1/2","3/4","7/8","15/16","31/32"]]},{"type":"formula","tex":"\\text{number of half-lives }n=\\text{ total time }\\div T\\tfrac{1}{2};\\quad \\text{ amount left }=\\text{ starting amount }\\times (1/2)^{n}","where":["⟪T\\tfrac{1}{2}⟫ = half-life (any time unit: s, min, h, days, years)","Use the SAME time unit for the total time and the half-life"],"label":"HALF-LIFE (Method 2)"},{"type":"h","text":"Correcting for background radiation"},{"type":"p","text":"A GM tube always picks up background radiation. Before working out a half-life, subtract the background count rate from every reading: **corrected count rate = measured count rate − background count rate**."},{"type":"warn","text":"Thinking that after two half-lives the sample is all gone (\"half + half = whole\"). After 2 half-lives a QUARTER is left. The amount halves each time and never quite reaches zero."},{"type":"tip","text":"Read the question carefully: does it ask for the amount LEFT (undecayed) or the amount that has DECAYED? Decayed = start − left."}],"examples":[{"id":"week12.12.5.e1","title":"Mass remaining","question":"Iodine-131 has a half-life of 8 days. A hospital receives 80 mg. How much iodine-131 is left after 32 days?","steps":[{"label":"Number of half-lives","text":"⟪n=32\\div 8=4⟫"},{"label":"Halving","text":"80 → 40 → 20 → 10 → 5 mg"},{"label":"Check","text":"⟪80\\times (1/2)^{4}=80\\div 16=5\\,\\mathrm{mg}⟫"}],"answer":"5 mg"},{"id":"week12.12.5.e2","title":"Finding the half-life","question":"The activity of a sample falls from 1600 Bq to 100 Bq in 24 hours. Calculate its half-life.","steps":[{"label":"Halving","text":"1600 → 800 → 400 → 200 → 100: that is 4 half-lives"},{"label":"Formula","text":"T½ = total time ÷ n"},{"label":"Substitute","text":"⟪T\\tfrac{1}{2}=24\\,\\mathrm{h}\\div 4⟫"}],"answer":"⟪T\\tfrac{1}{2}=6⟫ hours"},{"id":"week12.12.5.e3","title":"With background radiation","question":"A student measures a count rate of 420 counts/min from a source. Ten minutes later it is 120 counts/min. The background count rate is 20 counts/min. Find the half-life.","steps":[{"label":"Correct the readings","text":"⟪420-20=400⟫ counts/min; ⟪120-20=100⟫ counts/min"},{"label":"Halving","text":"400 → 200 → 100: 2 half-lives"},{"label":"Calculate","text":"⟪T\\tfrac{1}{2}=10\\,\\mathrm{min}\\div 2=5\\,\\mathrm{min}⟫"}],"answer":"5 minutes. (Without correcting, 420 → 120 is not a whole number of halvings, which is a clue you forgot the background.)"},{"id":"week12.12.5.e4","title":"Fraction decayed","question":"What fraction of a radioactive sample has decayed after 3 half-lives? What percentage is this?","steps":[{"label":"Fraction left","text":"⟪(1/2)^{3}=1/8⟫"},{"label":"Fraction decayed","text":"⟪1-1/8=7/8⟫"},{"label":"Percentage","text":"⟪7/8\\times 100=87.5\\%⟫"}],"answer":"7/8 (87.5%) has decayed; 1/8 (12.5%) remains."}]},"tryit":[{"id":"week12.12.5.p1","kind":"self","q":"Define half-life.","marks":1,"level":1,"lines":2,"scheme":"The time taken for half of the radioactive nuclei in a sample to decay / for the activity to fall to half its value (1).","points":[{"text":"The time taken for half of the radioactive nuclei in a sample to decay / for the activity to fall to half its value","marks":1}],"section":"try","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Mass remaining). Start like this: Number of half-lives: ⟪n=32\\div 8=4⟫","from":1}},{"id":"week12.12.5.p2","kind":"self","q":"What is meant by saying radioactive decay is \"random\"?","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"It is impossible to predict which nucleus will decay next (1) or when a particular nucleus will decay (1).","points":[{"text":"It is impossible to predict which nucleus will decay next","marks":1},{"text":"or when a particular nucleus will decay","marks":1}],"section":"try","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 4 (Fraction decayed). Start like this: Fraction left: ⟪(1/2)^{3}=1/8⟫","from":4}}],"practice":[{"id":"week12.12.5.p3","kind":"self","q":"A student heats a radioactive source in a flame. State and explain what happens to its half-life.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"No change (1); decay takes place in the nucleus and is not affected by temperature (or other physical/chemical conditions) (1).","points":[{"text":"No change","marks":1},{"text":"decay takes place in the nucleus and is not affected by temperature (or other physical/chemical conditions)","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 3 (With background radiation). Start like this: Correct the readings: ⟪420-20=400⟫ counts/min; ⟪120-20=100⟫ counts/min","from":3}},{"id":"week12.12.5.p4","kind":"num","q":"A sample contains 64 g of a radioisotope with a half-life of 3 hours. What mass of the isotope is left after 12 hours?","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"⟪12\\div 3=4⟫ half-lives (1); 64 → 32 → 16 → 8 → 4 g (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪12\\div 3=4⟫ half-lives","marks":1},{"text":"64 → 32 → 16 → 8 → 4 g","marks":1}],"num":{"value":4,"sf":1,"unit":"g","unitTex":"\\mathrm{g}"},"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Mass remaining). Start like this: Number of half-lives: ⟪n=32\\div 8=4⟫","from":1}},{"id":"week12.12.5.p5","kind":"num","q":"The count rate of a source falls from 3200 counts/min to 200 counts/min in 2 hours. Find its half-life in minutes.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"⟪3200\\rightarrow 1600\\rightarrow 800\\rightarrow 400\\rightarrow 200=4⟫ half-lives (1); ⟪2\\,\\mathrm{h}=120\\,\\mathrm{min}⟫ (1); ⟪T\\tfrac{1}{2}=120\\div 4=30\\,\\mathrm{min}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪3200\\rightarrow 1600\\rightarrow 800\\rightarrow 400\\rightarrow 200=4⟫ half-lives","marks":1},{"text":"⟪2\\,\\mathrm{h}=120\\,\\mathrm{min}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪T\\tfrac{1}{2}=120\\div 4=30\\,\\mathrm{min}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":30,"sf":1,"unit":"min","unitTex":"\\mathrm{min}"},"conf":"low","section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 3 (With background radiation). Start like this: Correct the readings: ⟪420-20=400⟫ counts/min; ⟪120-20=100⟫ counts/min","from":3}},{"id":"week12.12.5.p6","kind":"self","q":"Technetium-99m has a half-life of 6 hours. How long does it take for its activity to fall to 1/8 of its starting value?","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"⟪1/8=(1/2)^{3}⟫, so 3 half-lives (1); ⟪3\\times 6=18⟫ hours (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪1/8=(1/2)^{3}⟫, so 3 half-lives","marks":1},{"text":"⟪3\\times 6=18⟫ hours","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 2 (Finding the half-life). Start like this: Halving: 1600 → 800 → 400 → 200 → 100: that is 4 half-lives","from":2}},{"id":"week12.12.5.p7","kind":"self","q":"What percentage of a sample remains after 4 half-lives?","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"⟪(1/2)^{4}=1/16⟫ (1); ⟪1/16\\times 100=6.25\\%⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪(1/2)^{4}=1/16⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪1/16\\times 100=6.25\\%⟫","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 4 (Fraction decayed). Start like this: Fraction left: ⟪(1/2)^{3}=1/8⟫","from":4}},{"id":"week12.12.5.p8","kind":"num","q":"A source gives a measured count rate of 260 counts/min. After 15 minutes the measured count rate is 50 counts/min. The background count rate is 20 counts/min. Calculate the half-life.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Corrected: ⟪260-20=240⟫; ⟪50-20=30⟫ (1); ⟪240\\rightarrow 120\\rightarrow 60\\rightarrow 30=3⟫ half-lives (1); ⟪T\\tfrac{1}{2}=15\\div 3=5.0\\,\\mathrm{min}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"Corrected: ⟪260-20=240⟫; ⟪50-20=30⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪240\\rightarrow 120\\rightarrow 60\\rightarrow 30=3⟫ half-lives","marks":1},{"text":"⟪T\\tfrac{1}{2}=15\\div 3=5.0\\,\\mathrm{min}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":5,"sf":2,"unit":"min","unitTex":"\\mathrm{min}"},"conf":"low","section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 3 (With background radiation). Start like this: Correct the readings: ⟪420-20=400⟫ counts/min; ⟪120-20=100⟫ counts/min","from":3}}],"exit":[{"id":"week12.12.5.p9","kind":"self","q":"A sample contains ⟪6.0\\times 10^{20}⟫ radioactive nuclei. The half-life is 2 days. (a) How many undecayed nuclei are left after 6 days? (b) How many have decayed?","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"(a) ⟪6\\div 2=3⟫ half-lives (1); ⟪6.0\\times 10^{20}\\div 8=7.5\\times 10^{19}⟫ (1); (b) ⟪6.0\\times 10^{20}-7.5\\times 10^{19}=5.25\\times 10^{20}\\approx 5.3\\times 10^{20}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"(a) ⟪6\\div 2=3⟫ half-lives","marks":1},{"text":"⟪6.0\\times 10^{20}\\div 8=7.5\\times 10^{19}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"(b) ⟪6.0\\times 10^{20}-7.5\\times 10^{19}=5.25\\times 10^{20}\\approx 5.3\\times 10^{20}⟫","marks":1}],"section":"exit"},{"id":"week12.12.5.p10","kind":"self","q":"In the dice model, 120 dice are thrown and those showing a six are removed each time. (a) About how many are left after the first throw? (b) Explain how this models radioactive decay, and why the results do not exactly match your prediction.","marks":4,"level":3,"lines":5,"scheme":"(a) ⟪120\\times 5/6=100⟫ (1); (b) we cannot predict which die will show a six, just as we cannot predict which nucleus will decay (1); a constant fraction (1/6) is removed each throw, like a constant fraction of nuclei decaying in each equal time (1); the process is random, so actual numbers vary about the expected value (1).","points":[{"text":"(a) ⟪120\\times 5/6=100⟫","marks":1},{"text":"(b) we cannot predict which die will show a six, just as we cannot predict which nucleus will decay","marks":1},{"text":"a constant fraction (1/6) is removed each throw, like a constant fraction of nuclei decaying in each equal time","marks":1},{"text":"the process is random, so actual numbers vary about the expected value","marks":1}],"section":"exit"}],"warmup":[{"id":"week12.12.5.w1","kind":"self","q":"State what happens to (a) the nucleon number and (b) the proton number of a nucleus when it emits an α particle.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"(a) Decreases by 4 (1); (b) decreases by 2 (1).","points":[{"text":"(a) Decreases by 4","marks":1},{"text":"(b) decreases by 2","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week12.12.4.p1"},{"id":"week12.12.5.w2","kind":"self","q":"Why does γ emission NOT change the element?","marks":1,"level":1,"lines":2,"scheme":"γ has no mass and no charge, so A and Z (number of protons) are unchanged (1).","points":[{"text":"γ has no mass and no charge, so A and Z (number of protons) are unchanged","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week12.12.4.p3"},{"id":"week12.12.5.w3","kind":"self","q":"Complete: ⟪{}^{210}_{84}Po\\rightarrow {}^{?}_{?}Pb+{}^{4}_{2}⟫He","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":1,"scheme":"⟪A=210-4=206⟫ (1); ⟪Z=84-2=82\\ (1)⟫: ^206^~82~Pb.","points":[{"text":"⟪A=210-4=206⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪Z=84-2=82⟫. : ^206^~82~Pb","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week12.12.4.p4"}],"n":97},{"id":"week12.12.6","kind":"unit","unit":"12.6","title":"Uses of Radioisotopes, ⟪E=mc^{2}⟫ and Nuclear Energy","titleText":"Uses of Radioisotopes, E = mc² and Nuclear Energy","goals":[{"code":"E3.11","text":"Describe uses of radioisotopes in medicine, industry, agriculture and carbon dating, and the precautions needed"},{"code":"E3.12","text":"Use ⟪E=mc^{2}⟫ to relate the energy released in nuclear reactions to the loss of mass (mass defect)"},{"code":"E3.13","text":"Discuss the arguments for and against the use of nuclear energy"}],"codes":["E3.11","E3.12","E3.13"],"words":[{"term":"Nucleus","def":"Tiny, dense, positive centre of an atom containing protons and neutrons."},{"term":"Isotopes","def":"Atoms of the same element with the same number of protons but different numbers of neutrons."},{"term":"Radioisotope","def":"An isotope with an unstable nucleus, which is radioactive."},{"term":"Ionisation","def":"Removing or adding electrons to atoms, making ions."},{"term":"Count rate","def":"Number of counts recorded by a detector per second or per minute."},{"term":"Activity","def":"Number of decays per second in a sample, measured in becquerels (Bq)."},{"term":"Half-life","def":"Time for half the radioactive nuclei in a sample to decay (activity to halve)."},{"term":"Mass defect","def":"Loss of mass in a nuclear reaction, converted into energy ⟪(E=mc^{2})⟫."},{"term":"Fission","def":"Splitting of a large nucleus into two smaller nuclei, releasing energy and neutrons."},{"term":"Fusion","def":"Joining of two light nuclei to form a heavier one, releasing energy (as in the Sun)."}],"learn":{"notes":[{"type":"h","text":"Uses of radioisotopes (E3.11)"},{"type":"p","text":"To choose an isotope for a job, ask two questions: **(1) Which radiation is needed?** (Does it have to get out of the body or through a pipe? Then γ. Does it have to be stopped by a thin sheet? Then α or β.) **(2) What half-life is needed?** (Inside a patient: short, hours or days, so it does not stay radioactive. In a smoke detector: long, years, so it lasts.)"},{"type":"table","headers":["Use","Isotope (radiation, T½)","How it works"],"rows":[["**Medical tracer** (e.g. checking the thyroid or kidneys)","Iodine-131 (β and γ, 8 days); technetium-99m (γ, 6 hours)","Swallowed or injected; it collects in the organ. A **γ camera** outside the body detects where it goes. Short half-life and γ (passes out of the body, little ionisation inside)"],["**Radiotherapy** (treating cancer)","Cobalt-60 (γ, 5.3 years)","Beams of γ rays are aimed at the tumour from different directions so the tumour gets a large dose and healthy tissue a small one. The γ rays kill the cancer cells."],["**Sterilising** medical equipment and food","Cobalt-60 (γ)","γ rays pass through the sealed packaging and kill bacteria. The items do NOT become radioactive."],["**Thickness gauge** (paper, plastic, metal foil)","Strontium-90 (β, 29 years)","β passes through the sheet to a detector. If the sheet gets too thick, the count rate falls and the rollers are adjusted. Long half-life so the count rate stays steady."],["**Leak detection** in underground pipes","A short-half-life γ emitter (e.g. sodium-24, 15 hours)","Added to the liquid in the pipe. A detector on the ground finds where radiation is higher: that is the leak."],["**Smoke detector**","Americium-241 (α, 432 years)","α ionises the air so a small current flows. Smoke absorbs the α, the current drops and the alarm sounds."],["**Agriculture**","Phosphorus-32 (β, 14 days)","Added to fertiliser to trace how plants take up phosphorus."],["**Carbon dating** (archaeology)","Carbon-14 (β, 5730 years)","See below."]]},{"type":"h","text":"Carbon dating"},{"type":"bullets","items":["Living plants take in carbon dioxide; animals eat plants. So all living things contain a fixed, small proportion of radioactive **carbon-14**.","When the organism **dies**, it stops taking in carbon. The carbon-14 decays and is not replaced, so its activity falls, halving every **5730 years**.","Compare the activity of carbon-14 in the old sample with that in the same mass of living material. Work out how many half-lives have passed.","Only works for things that were once **living** (wood, bone, cloth, charcoal), and up to about 50 000 years old."]},{"type":"h","text":"Dangers and precautions"},{"type":"p","text":"Ionising radiation damages living cells. It can kill cells (radiation burns, radiation sickness) or damage DNA, which can cause **cancer** or mutations. Outside the body, γ and β are the most dangerous (they penetrate the skin). INSIDE the body (swallowed or breathed in), α is the most dangerous because it is the most ionising."},{"type":"bullets","items":["Keep **time** of exposure short.","Keep a large **distance** (use tongs, remote handling).","Use **shielding**: lead-lined containers, lead aprons, concrete walls.","Workers wear a **film badge** (dosimeter) to record their dose.","Label sources with the radiation hazard symbol; store them locked away; dispose of waste safely."]},{"type":"h","text":"⟪E=mc^{2}⟫: mass and energy (E3.12)"},{"type":"p","text":"Einstein showed that mass is a very concentrated form of energy. In a nuclear reaction the total mass of the particles AFTER is slightly LESS than the total mass BEFORE. This missing mass is the **mass defect**, and it has been changed into energy (kinetic energy of the particles and γ rays)."},{"type":"formula","tex":"E=mc^{2}","where":["⟪E⟫ = energy released, in joules (J)","⟪m⟫ = mass converted (mass defect), in kilograms (kg)","⟪c⟫ = speed of light ⟪=3.0\\times 10^{8}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫, so ⟪c^{2}=9.0\\times 10^{16}\\,\\mathrm{m}^{2}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-2}⟫"],"label":"MASS–ENERGY EQUATION"},{"type":"p","text":"Because c^2^ is enormous, a tiny mass gives a huge energy: just 1 g (0.001 kg) gives ⟪9.0\\times 10^{13}\\,\\mathrm{J}⟫, about the energy from burning 2000 tonnes of oil."},{"type":"table","headers":["","Nuclear fission","Nuclear fusion"],"rows":[["What happens","A large nucleus (e.g. uranium-235) is hit by a neutron and **splits** into two smaller nuclei plus 2–3 neutrons","Two very small nuclei (e.g. hydrogen isotopes) **join** to make a bigger nucleus (helium)"],["Where","Nuclear power stations, atomic bombs","The Sun and stars; hydrogen bomb"],["Energy from","Mass defect, ⟪E=mc^{2}⟫","Mass defect, ⟪E=mc^{2}⟫"],["Chain reaction","The neutrons released split more nuclei","Needs very high temperature and pressure"]]},{"type":"warn","text":"Using grams in ⟪E=mc^{2}⟫. Mass MUST be in kilograms: ⟪2\\,\\mathrm{g}=0.002\\,\\mathrm{kg}=2\\times 10^{-3}\\,\\mathrm{kg}⟫. Also remember to SQUARE c: ⟪c^{2}=9.0\\times 10^{16}⟫, not ⟪3.0\\times 10^{8}⟫."},{"type":"h","text":"Nuclear energy: for and against (E3.13)"},{"type":"table","headers":["Arguments FOR nuclear power","Arguments AGAINST nuclear power"],"rows":[["Produces **no carbon dioxide** while running, so it does not add to global warming or climate change (a big issue for low-lying Caribbean islands)","Produces **radioactive waste** that stays dangerous for thousands of years and must be stored safely"],["A **very small mass of fuel** gives a huge amount of energy ⟪(E=mc^{2})⟫","Risk of a serious **accident** releasing radiation over a wide area (Chernobyl 1986, Fukushima 2011)"],["Reliable: works day and night in any weather, unlike solar and wind","**Very expensive** to build and to decommission (shut down safely) at the end of its life"],["Reduces dependence on imported oil and gas","Fuel (uranium) must be imported; risk of materials being used for **weapons** or terrorism"],["Fuel reserves will last a long time","Caribbean risks: hurricanes, earthquakes and volcanoes could damage a plant; small islands have little space for safe waste storage or evacuation"]]},{"type":"tip","text":"\"Discuss\" questions need points on BOTH sides and a short conclusion. Aim for at least two FOR and two AGAINST, each written as a full reason (not one word)."}],"examples":[{"id":"week12.12.6.e1","title":"Carbon dating","question":"A piece of wood from an old canoe has a carbon-14 activity that is 1/4 of the activity of the same mass of living wood. The half-life of carbon-14 is 5730 years. How old is the canoe?","steps":[{"label":"Half-lives","text":"1 → 1/2 → 1/4 is 2 half-lives"},{"label":"Formula","text":"⟪age=n\\times T\\tfrac{1}{2}⟫"},{"label":"Substitute","text":"⟪age=2\\times 5730⟫"}],"answer":"11 460 years (about 11 500 years)."},{"id":"week12.12.6.e2","title":"Energy from mass","question":"In a nuclear reactor, 2.0 g of mass is converted into energy. Calculate the energy released. ⟪(c=3.0\\times 10^{8}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1})⟫","steps":[{"label":"Convert","text":"⟪m=2.0\\,\\mathrm{g}=2.0\\times 10^{-3}\\,\\mathrm{kg}⟫"},{"label":"Formula","text":"⟪E=mc^{2}⟫"},{"label":"Substitute","text":"⟪E=2.0\\times 10^{-3}\\times (3.0\\times 10^{8})^{2}=2.0\\times 10^{-3}\\times 9.0\\times 10^{16}⟫"}],"answer":"⟪E=1.8\\times 10^{14}\\,\\mathrm{J}⟫"},{"id":"week12.12.6.e3","title":"Finding the mass defect","question":"One fission of a uranium-235 nucleus releases ⟪3.2\\times 10^{-11}\\,\\mathrm{J}⟫. Calculate the mass defect for one fission.","steps":[{"label":"Formula","text":"⟪m=E\\div c^{2}⟫"},{"label":"Substitute","text":"⟪m=3.2\\times 10^{-11}\\div 9.0\\times 10^{16}⟫"},{"label":"Calculate","text":"⟪m=3.6\\times 10^{-28}\\,\\mathrm{kg}⟫"}],"answer":"3.6 × 10^−28^ kg (2 s.f.)"},{"id":"week12.12.6.e4","title":"Choosing an isotope","question":"A doctor wants a radioisotope to inject into a patient to find a blockage in the kidneys. Should she use an α emitter with a half-life of 6 hours, a γ emitter with a half-life of 6 hours, or a γ emitter with a half-life of 30 years? Explain.","steps":[{"label":"Radiation","text":"it must be detected OUTSIDE the body, so it must pass through tissue: γ. An α emitter would not get out and would badly ionise (damage) the cells."},{"label":"Half-life","text":"short enough to decay soon after the test (30 years would keep the patient radioactive), long enough to finish the test: 6 hours."}],"answer":"The γ emitter with a half-life of 6 hours."}]},"tryit":[{"id":"week12.12.6.p1","kind":"self","q":"Give ONE use of radioisotopes in (a) medicine, (b) industry.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"(a) Any one: tracers to study organs; radiotherapy to kill cancer cells; sterilising instruments (1). (b) Any one: thickness gauges; leak detection; smoke detectors; sterilising food (1).","points":[{"text":"(a) Any one: tracers to study organs; radiotherapy to kill cancer cells; sterilising instruments","marks":1},{"text":"(b) Any one: thickness gauges; leak detection; smoke detectors; sterilising food","marks":1}],"section":"try"},{"id":"week12.12.6.p2","kind":"self","q":"Explain why a γ emitter, and not an α emitter, is used as a medical tracer.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"γ can pass out of the body to be detected by a camera/detector outside (1); α would be absorbed inside the body and, being strongly ionising, would damage cells (1).","points":[{"text":"γ can pass out of the body to be detected by a camera/detector outside","marks":1},{"text":"α would be absorbed inside the body and, being strongly ionising, would damage cells","marks":1}],"section":"try","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 4 (Choosing an isotope). Start like this: Radiation: it must be detected OUTSIDE the body, so it must pass through tissue: γ. An α emitter would not get out and would badly ionise (damage) the cells.","from":4}}],"practice":[{"id":"week12.12.6.p3","kind":"self","q":"A thickness gauge in a paper factory uses a β source. Explain why β is used and not α or γ.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"α would be stopped completely by the paper (1); γ would pass through almost unchanged whatever the thickness (1); the amount of β getting through changes noticeably with small changes in thickness (1).","points":[{"text":"α would be stopped completely by the paper","marks":1},{"text":"γ would pass through almost unchanged whatever the thickness","marks":1},{"text":"the amount of β getting through changes noticeably with small changes in thickness","marks":1}],"section":"practice"},{"id":"week12.12.6.p4","kind":"self","q":"Explain why carbon dating cannot be used to find the age of a rock.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"Carbon dating only works on things that were once living (1), because they took in carbon-14 while alive; rocks never did (1).","points":[{"text":"Carbon dating only works on things that were once living","marks":1},{"text":"because they took in carbon-14 while alive; rocks never did","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Carbon dating). Start like this: Half-lives: 1 → 1/2 → 1/4 is 2 half-lives","from":1}},{"id":"week12.12.6.p5","kind":"self","q":"A bone has a carbon-14 activity of 1/8 of that in living bone. The half-life of carbon-14 is 5730 years. How old is the bone?","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"⟪1/8=3⟫ half-lives (1); ⟪3\\times 5730=17\\,190\\,\\mathrm{years}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪1/8=3⟫ half-lives","marks":1},{"text":"⟪3\\times 5730=17\\,190\\,\\mathrm{years}⟫","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Carbon dating). Start like this: Half-lives: 1 → 1/2 → 1/4 is 2 half-lives","from":1}},{"id":"week12.12.6.p6","kind":"self","q":"State what is meant by \"mass defect\".","marks":1,"level":1,"lines":2,"scheme":"The decrease in total mass in a nuclear reaction (mass of products is less than the mass of reactants), which is converted into energy (1).","points":[{"text":"The decrease in total mass in a nuclear reaction (mass of products is less than the mass of reactants), which is converted into energy","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 3 (Finding the mass defect). Start like this: Formula: ⟪m=E\\div c^{2}⟫","from":3}},{"id":"week12.12.6.p7","kind":"num","q":"Calculate the energy released when 0.50 kg of mass is converted completely into energy. ⟪(c=3.0\\times 10^{8}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1})⟫","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"⟪E=mc^{2}=0.50\\times (3.0\\times 10^{8})^{2}⟫ (1) ⟪=4.5\\times 10^{16}\\,\\mathrm{J}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪E=mc^{2}=0.50\\times (3.0\\times 10^{8})^{2}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=4.5\\times 10^{16}\\,\\mathrm{J}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":45000000000000000,"sf":2,"unit":"J","unitTex":"\\mathrm{J}"},"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 2 (Energy from mass). Start like this: Convert: ⟪m=2.0\\,\\mathrm{g}=2.0\\times 10^{-3}\\,\\mathrm{kg}⟫","from":2}},{"id":"week12.12.6.p8","kind":"num","q":"In a fusion reaction in the Sun, the mass decreases by ⟪3.1\\times 10^{-29}\\,\\mathrm{kg}⟫. Calculate the energy released.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"⟪E=3.1\\times 10^{-29}\\times 9.0\\times 10^{16}⟫ (1) ⟪=2.79\\times 10^{-12}\\approx 2.8\\times 10^{-12}\\,\\mathrm{J}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪E=3.1\\times 10^{-29}\\times 9.0\\times 10^{16}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=2.79\\times 10^{-12}\\approx 2.8\\times 10^{-12}\\,\\mathrm{J}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":2.8e-12,"sf":2,"unit":"J","unitTex":"\\mathrm{J}"},"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 2 (Energy from mass). Start like this: Convert: ⟪m=2.0\\,\\mathrm{g}=2.0\\times 10^{-3}\\,\\mathrm{kg}⟫","from":2}}],"exit":[{"id":"week12.12.6.p9","kind":"num","q":"A power station needs ⟪3.6\\times 10^{15}\\,\\mathrm{J}⟫ of energy. What mass must be converted into energy to supply this?","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"⟪m=E\\div c^{2}=3.6\\times 10^{15}\\div 9.0\\times 10^{16}⟫ (1) ⟪=0.040\\,\\mathrm{kg}\\ (40\\,\\mathrm{g})⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪m=E\\div c^{2}=3.6\\times 10^{15}\\div 9.0\\times 10^{16}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=0.040\\,\\mathrm{kg}\\ (40\\,\\mathrm{g})⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":0.04,"sf":2,"unit":"kg","unitTex":"\\mathrm{kg}","alt":[40]},"section":"exit"},{"id":"week12.12.6.p10","kind":"self","q":"A Caribbean government is thinking of building a nuclear power station. Give TWO arguments for and TWO arguments against.","marks":4,"level":3,"lines":6,"scheme":"For (any two): no CO~2~/greenhouse gases (1); large energy from small mass of fuel / reliable in all weather / less imported oil (1). Against (any two): long-lived radioactive waste (1); risk of accident (worsened by hurricanes/earthquakes), high cost, weapons risk (1).","points":[{"text":"For (any two): no CO~2~/greenhouse gases","marks":1},{"text":"large energy from small mass of fuel / reliable in all weather / less imported oil","marks":1},{"text":"Against (any two): long-lived radioactive waste","marks":1},{"text":"risk of accident (worsened by hurricanes/earthquakes), high cost, weapons risk","marks":1}],"section":"exit"}],"warmup":[{"id":"week12.12.6.w1","kind":"self","q":"What is meant by saying radioactive decay is \"random\"?","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"It is impossible to predict which nucleus will decay next (1) or when a particular nucleus will decay (1).","points":[{"text":"It is impossible to predict which nucleus will decay next","marks":1},{"text":"or when a particular nucleus will decay","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week12.12.5.p2"},{"id":"week12.12.6.w2","kind":"self","q":"A student heats a radioactive source in a flame. State and explain what happens to its half-life.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"No change (1); decay takes place in the nucleus and is not affected by temperature (or other physical/chemical conditions) (1).","points":[{"text":"No change","marks":1},{"text":"decay takes place in the nucleus and is not affected by temperature (or other physical/chemical conditions)","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week12.12.5.p3"},{"id":"week12.12.6.w3","kind":"num","q":"A sample contains 64 g of a radioisotope with a half-life of 3 hours. What mass of the isotope is left after 12 hours?","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"⟪12\\div 3=4⟫ half-lives (1); 64 → 32 → 16 → 8 → 4 g (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪12\\div 3=4⟫ half-lives","marks":1},{"text":"64 → 32 → 16 → 8 → 4 g","marks":1}],"num":{"value":4,"sf":1,"unit":"g","unitTex":"\\mathrm{g}"},"section":"warmup","src":"week12.12.5.p4"}],"n":98},{"id":"week12.check","kind":"check","title":"Book check","titleText":"Book check","check":true,"watchOut":["Mixing up A and Z. The top (bigger) number is the nucleon number A; the bottom number is the proton number Z. Neutrons ⟪=A-Z⟫.","Saying α particles \"hit\" the gold nucleus. They are REPELLED by it (like charges repel) without touching.","Saying lead \"stops\" gamma. It only REDUCES (absorbs most of) gamma.","Saying α is deflected more than β. β bends MORE (much smaller mass) and in the opposite direction.","In β decay, decreasing Z or writing the β particle as ^0^~1~e. Z goes UP by 1 and the β particle is ^0^~−1~e.","Unbalanced nuclear equations. Always check: top numbers equal on both sides, bottom numbers equal on both sides.","Thinking two half-lives means the sample is all gone. After 2 half-lives, 1/4 is left; after 3, 1/8.","Forgetting to subtract background radiation before finding a half-life from count rates.","Using grams instead of kilograms in ⟪E=mc^{2}⟫, or forgetting to square ⟪c\\ (c^{2}=9.0\\times 10^{16})⟫.","Saying heating or cooling changes the half-life. Decay is not affected by physical or chemical conditions."],"mcq":[{"id":"week12.check.m1","kind":"mcq","q":"Which observation in the Geiger–Marsden experiment showed that the atom is mostly empty space?","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["A few α particles bounced back","Most α particles passed straight through the foil","Some α particles were deflected through small angles","The foil became radioactive"],"answer":"B","explanation":"If most α particles meet nothing, most of the atom is empty. A is the evidence for a tiny, dense nucleus, not for empty space.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week12.check.m2","kind":"mcq","q":"Who discovered the neutron?","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["Thomson","Rutherford","Bohr","Chadwick"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Chadwick discovered the neutron in 1932. Rutherford (B) proposed the nucleus but did not discover the neutron; Thomson discovered the electron.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week12.check.m3","kind":"mcq","q":"Compared with a proton, an electron has","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["the same mass and the same charge","a much smaller mass and an equal but opposite charge","a much smaller mass and a smaller charge","the same mass and an opposite charge"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Charges are equal in size ⟪(1.6\\times 10^{-19}\\,\\mathrm{C})⟫ but opposite in sign; the electron is about 1840 times lighter. C is wrong because the size of the charge is the same.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week12.check.m4","kind":"mcq","q":"An atom of an element is written ^59^~27~Co. How many neutrons does it have?","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["27","59","32","86"],"answer":"C","explanation":"⟪N=A-Z=59-27=32⟫. A (27) is the number of protons; D adds the numbers instead of subtracting.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week12.check.m5","kind":"mcq","q":"Isotopes of an element have the same number of","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["neutrons but different numbers of protons","nucleons but different numbers of protons","protons but different numbers of neutrons","protons and neutrons but different numbers of electrons"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Same element = same proton number; different isotopes = different neutron numbers. A describes different elements.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week12.check.m6","kind":"mcq","q":"An element has the electron arrangement 2,8,3. In which group and period of the Periodic Table is it?","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["Group III, Period 3","Group III, Period 2","Group II, Period 3","Group VIII, Period 3"],"answer":"A","explanation":"3 outer electrons → Group III; 3 occupied shells → Period 3. B wrongly counts only the full shells for the period.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week12.check.m7","kind":"mcq","q":"Which radiation is a helium nucleus?","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["α","β","γ","X-ray"],"answer":"A","explanation":"An α particle is 2 protons + 2 neutrons, a helium nucleus. β (B) is a fast electron.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week12.check.m8","kind":"mcq","q":"A source is placed in front of a GM tube. The count rate falls to background when 5 mm of aluminium is placed in between, but does not change when paper is used. The source emits","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["α only","β only","γ only","α and γ"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Paper makes no difference, so no α; stopped by aluminium, so β and no γ (γ would get through). D is wrong because paper would have reduced an α count.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week12.check.m9","kind":"mcq","q":"In a cloud chamber, which radiation produces short, thick, straight tracks?","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["α","β","γ","all three"],"answer":"A","explanation":"α is strongly ionising (thick track) and heavy (straight), with a short range. β tracks (B) are thin and wavy.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week12.check.m10","kind":"mcq","q":"A beam of α, β and γ radiation passes between two charged plates. Which statement is correct?","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["α bends towards the positive plate","β bends towards the negative plate","γ bends towards the positive plate","β bends more than α"],"answer":"D","explanation":"β is much lighter, so it bends more (towards the + plate). A is wrong: positive α is attracted to the negative plate.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week12.check.m11","kind":"mcq","q":"Polonium-210 (^210^~84~Po) emits an α particle. The new nucleus is","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["^206^~82~Pb","^210^~85~At","^206^~84~Po","^208^~82~Pb"],"answer":"A","explanation":"α decay: ⟪A-4=206⟫, ⟪Z-2=82⟫. B is the result of β decay; D subtracts only 2 from A.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week12.check.m12","kind":"mcq","q":"In β decay, which of these happens inside the nucleus?","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["A proton changes into a neutron","Two protons and two neutrons are emitted","A neutron changes into a proton and an electron","The nucleus loses energy but not mass or charge"],"answer":"C","explanation":"A neutron becomes a proton (Z rises by 1) and the electron is emitted. B describes α decay; D describes γ emission.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week12.check.m13","kind":"mcq","q":"A radioactive sample has a half-life of 10 minutes. What fraction of the sample is left after 30 minutes?","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["1/3","1/4","1/6","1/8"],"answer":"D","explanation":"⟪30\\div 10=3⟫ half-lives, so ⟪(1/2)^{3}=1/8⟫. A wrongly divides by the number of half-lives instead of halving each time.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week12.check.m14","kind":"mcq","q":"Which of the following changes the half-life of a radioactive isotope?","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["Heating it","Increasing the pressure","Putting it in a chemical compound","None of these"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Decay takes place in the nucleus and is not affected by physical or chemical conditions, so the half-life is fixed.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week12.check.m15","kind":"mcq","q":"In a nuclear reaction, the mass decreases by ⟪2.0\\times 10^{-28}\\,\\mathrm{kg}⟫. The energy released is ⟪(c=3.0\\times 10^{8}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1})⟫","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["⟪6.0\\times 10^{-20}\\,\\mathrm{J}⟫","⟪1.8\\times 10^{-11}\\,\\mathrm{J}⟫","⟪6.0\\times 10^{-11}\\,\\mathrm{J}⟫","⟪1.8\\times 10^{-19}\\,\\mathrm{J}⟫"],"answer":"B","explanation":"⟪E=mc^{2}=2.0\\times 10^{-28}\\times 9.0\\times 10^{16}=1.8\\times 10^{-11}\\,\\mathrm{J}⟫. A forgets to square c (uses mc).","section":"p01"}],"structured":[{"id":"week12.check.s1","kind":"multi","title":"Structure of the atom","total":12,"stem":["The nuclear model of the atom was developed from the results of the Geiger–Marsden experiment."],"stemBlocks":[],"parts":[{"id":"week12.check.s1.a","kind":"self","q":"Describe the Geiger–Marsden experiment. Include what was fired, what the target was, and how the particles were detected.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":4,"scheme":"A beam of α particles (1) was fired at a thin gold foil in a vacuum (1); a movable zinc sulfide screen/detector counted the α particles scattered at different angles (1).","points":[{"text":"A beam of α particles","marks":1},{"text":"was fired at a thin gold foil in a vacuum","marks":1},{"text":"a movable zinc sulfide screen/detector counted the α particles scattered at different angles","marks":1}],"label":"(a)"},{"id":"week12.check.s1.b","kind":"self","q":"State TWO observations and explain what each tells us about the atom.","marks":4,"level":3,"lines":5,"scheme":"Most went straight through (1) → the atom is mostly empty space (1); a very few bounced back (1) → the positive charge and mass are concentrated in a tiny, dense nucleus (1). (Accept: some deflected → nucleus positive.)","points":[{"text":"Most went straight through","marks":1},{"text":"→ the atom is mostly empty space","marks":1},{"text":"a very few bounced back","marks":1},{"text":"→ the positive charge and mass are concentrated in a tiny, dense nucleus (Accept: some deflected → nucleus positive.)","marks":1}],"label":"(b)"},{"id":"week12.check.s1.c","kind":"self","q":"An atom of sodium is represented by ^23^~11~Na. State the number of protons, neutrons and electrons in a neutral atom, and give its electron arrangement.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"11 protons and 11 electrons (1); ⟪23-11=12⟫ neutrons (1); 2,8,1 (1).","points":[{"text":"11 protons and 11 electrons","marks":1},{"text":"⟪23-11=12⟫ neutrons","marks":1},{"text":"2,8,1","marks":1}],"label":"(c)"},{"id":"week12.check.s1.d","kind":"self","q":"Sodium-24 (^24^~11~Na) is another isotope of sodium. State ONE way it is the same as sodium-23 and ONE way it is different.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"Same: number of protons (11) / electrons / chemical properties (1); different: number of neutrons (13 instead of 12) / nucleon number / mass (also Na-24 is radioactive) (1).","points":[{"text":"Same: number of protons (11) / electrons / chemical properties","marks":1},{"text":"different: number of neutrons (13 instead of 12) / nucleon number / mass (also Na-24 is radioactive)","marks":1}],"label":"(d)"}]},{"id":"week12.check.s2","kind":"multi","title":"Alpha, beta and gamma radiation","total":13,"stem":["A radioactive source emits α, β and γ radiation."],"stemBlocks":[],"parts":[{"id":"week12.check.s2.a","kind":"self","q":"Copy and complete the table for α, β and γ: nature, charge, and a material that stops (or greatly reduces) it.","marks":6,"level":3,"lines":5,"scheme":"α: helium nucleus (1), +2, paper (1); β: fast electron (1), −1, few mm aluminium (1); γ: electromagnetic wave (1), 0, thick lead/concrete (1).","points":[{"text":"α: helium nucleus","marks":1},{"text":"+2, paper","marks":1},{"text":"β: fast electron","marks":1},{"text":"−1, few mm aluminium","marks":1},{"text":"γ: electromagnetic wave","marks":1},{"text":"0, thick lead/concrete","marks":1}],"label":"(a)"},{"id":"week12.check.s2.b","kind":"self","q":"The radiation passes through a magnetic field. Explain why γ is not deflected and why β is deflected more than α.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":4,"scheme":"γ has no charge, so there is no force on it (1); β has a much smaller mass than α (1), so the same kind of force produces a much larger deflection (β is also deflected in the opposite direction because it is negative) (1).","points":[{"text":"γ has no charge, so there is no force on it","marks":1},{"text":"β has a much smaller mass than α","marks":1},{"text":"so the same kind of force produces a much larger deflection (β is also deflected in the opposite direction because it is negative)","marks":1}],"label":"(b)"},{"id":"week12.check.s2.c","kind":"self","q":"Uranium-234 (^234^~92~U) decays by α emission to thorium (Th). Write a balanced nuclear equation.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"⟪{}^{234}_{92}U\\rightarrow {}^{230}_{90}Th+{}^{4}_{2}⟫He: correct Th numbers (1); α symbol and balance (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪{}^{234}_{92}U\\rightarrow {}^{230}_{90}Th+{}^{4}_{2}⟫He: correct Th numbers","marks":1},{"text":"α symbol and balance","marks":1}],"label":"(c)"},{"id":"week12.check.s2.d","kind":"self","q":"Explain why α radiation is the most dangerous if a radioactive substance is swallowed.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"α is the most strongly ionising radiation (1); inside the body all its energy is absorbed in nearby cells, causing great damage (e.g. cancer) (1).","points":[{"text":"α is the most strongly ionising radiation","marks":1},{"text":"inside the body all its energy is absorbed in nearby cells, causing great damage (e.g. cancer)","marks":1}],"label":"(d)"}]},{"id":"week12.check.s3","kind":"multi","title":"Half-life, uses and mass–energy","total":14,"stem":["Cobalt-60 is a γ emitter with a half-life of 5.3 years. It is used in hospitals for radiotherapy and for sterilising equipment."],"stemBlocks":[],"parts":[{"id":"week12.check.s3.a","kind":"self","q":"Define half-life.","marks":1,"level":1,"lines":2,"scheme":"Time taken for half the radioactive nuclei in a sample to decay / for the activity to halve (1).","points":[{"text":"Time taken for half the radioactive nuclei in a sample to decay / for the activity to halve","marks":1}],"label":"(a)"},{"id":"week12.check.s3.b","kind":"self","q":"A new cobalt-60 source has an activity of ⟪4.0\\times 10^{12}\\,\\mathrm{Bq}⟫. Calculate its activity after 15.9 years.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"⟪15.9\\div 5.3=3⟫ half-lives (1); ⟪4.0\\times 10^{12}\\div 8⟫ (1) ⟪=5.0\\times 10^{11}\\,\\mathrm{Bq}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪15.9\\div 5.3=3⟫ half-lives","marks":1},{"text":"⟪4.0\\times 10^{12}\\div 8⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=5.0\\times 10^{11}\\,\\mathrm{Bq}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":500000000000,"sf":2,"unit":"Bq","unitTex":"\\mathrm{Bq}"},"label":"(b)","autoKind":"num"},{"id":"week12.check.s3.c","kind":"self","q":"Explain why γ rays are suitable for sterilising medical instruments sealed in plastic bags.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"γ is very penetrating, so it passes through the sealed packaging (1) and kills bacteria on the instruments without making them radioactive / without opening the bags (1).","points":[{"text":"γ is very penetrating, so it passes through the sealed packaging","marks":1},{"text":"and kills bacteria on the instruments without making them radioactive / without opening the bags","marks":1}],"label":"(c)"},{"id":"week12.check.s3.d","kind":"self","q":"State TWO precautions that hospital workers should take when handling cobalt-60.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"Any two: store/use behind thick lead or concrete shielding; handle remotely/with tongs; minimise exposure time; keep distance; wear a film badge/dosimeter (1)(1).","points":[{"text":"Any two: store/use behind thick lead or concrete shielding; handle remotely/with tongs; minimise exposure time; keep distance; wear a film badge/dosimeter","marks":2}],"label":"(d)"},{"id":"week12.check.s3.e","kind":"self","q":"When a nucleus decays, the mass of the products is slightly less than the mass of the original nucleus. Explain what happens to this mass.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"The missing mass (mass defect) (1) is converted into energy (kinetic energy of the particles and γ radiation) according to ⟪E=mc^{2}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"The missing mass (mass defect)","marks":1},{"text":"is converted into energy (kinetic energy of the particles and γ radiation) according to ⟪E=mc^{2}⟫","marks":1}],"label":"(e)"},{"id":"week12.check.s3.f","kind":"self","q":"In one decay the mass defect is ⟪5.0\\times 10^{-30}\\,\\mathrm{kg}⟫. Calculate the energy released. ⟪(c=3.0\\times 10^{8}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1})⟫","marks":4,"level":3,"lines":4,"scheme":"⟪E=mc^{2}⟫ (1); ⟪E=5.0\\times 10^{-30}\\times (3.0\\times 10^{8})^{2}⟫ (1); ⟪=5.0\\times 10^{-30}\\times 9.0\\times 10^{16}⟫ (1); ⟪=4.5\\times 10^{-13}\\,\\mathrm{J}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪E=mc^{2}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪E=5.0\\times 10^{-30}\\times (3.0\\times 10^{8})^{2}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=5.0\\times 10^{-30}\\times 9.0\\times 10^{16}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=4.5\\times 10^{-13}\\,\\mathrm{J}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":4.5e-13,"sf":2,"unit":"J","unitTex":"\\mathrm{J}"},"conf":"low","label":"(f)","autoKind":"num"}]}],"n":99},{"id":"week12.past","kind":"past","title":"Past-paper practice","titleText":"Past-paper practice","check":true,"log":true,"questions":[{"id":"week12.past.q1","kind":"multi","title":"Data analysis (Paper 02 Question 1 style)","total":21,"stem":["A student used a GM tube and counter to measure the count rate from a radioactive source every 2 minutes. Before starting, she measured the background count rate as 20 counts per minute. Her results are shown below.","Corrected count rate = measured count rate − background count rate."],"stemBlocks":[{"type":"table","headers":["Time, t / min","0","2","4","6","8","10","12"],"rows":[["Measured count rate / counts min^−1^","820","530","335","222","144","101","70"],["Corrected count rate / counts min^−1^","800","510","","202","124","","50"]]}],"parts":[{"id":"week12.past.q1.a","kind":"self","q":"What is meant by \"background radiation\"? State TWO sources of it.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Radiation always present in the surroundings, not from the source being tested (1); any two sources, e.g. rocks/soil/radon, cosmic rays, buildings, food (1).","points":[{"text":"Radiation always present in the surroundings, not from the source being tested","marks":1},{"text":"any two sources, e.g. rocks/soil/radon, cosmic rays, buildings, food","marks":1}],"label":"(a)"},{"id":"week12.past.q1.b","kind":"self","q":"Complete the table by calculating the corrected count rate at ⟪t=4\\,\\mathrm{min}⟫ and ⟪t=10\\,\\mathrm{min}⟫.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"⟪335-20=315⟫ (1); ⟪101-20=81⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪335-20=315⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪101-20=81⟫","marks":1}],"label":"(b)"},{"id":"week12.past.q1.c","kind":"self","q":"On the grid, plot a graph of corrected count rate (y-axis) against time (x-axis). Draw a smooth curve through the points.","marks":6,"level":3,"img":{"src":"img/week12/graph_paper","alt":"On the grid, plot a graph of corrected count rate (y-axis) against time (x-axis). Draw a smooth curve through the points.","w":1174,"h":934},"lines":0,"scheme":"Axes correct way round and labelled with quantity and unit (1); suitable scale using most of the grid, e.g. 1 large square ⟪=1\\,\\mathrm{min}⟫ and 100 counts min^−1^ (1); all 7 points plotted correctly (3; −1 per error); smooth curve (not dot-to-dot, not straight lines) (1).","points":[{"text":"Axes correct way round and labelled with quantity and unit","marks":1},{"text":"suitable scale using most of the grid, e.g. 1 large square ⟪=1\\,\\mathrm{min}⟫ and 100 counts min^−1^","marks":1},{"text":"all 7 points plotted correctly","marks":3},{"text":"smooth curve (not dot-to-dot, not straight lines)","marks":1}],"label":"(c)","grid":true},{"id":"week12.past.q1.d","kind":"self","q":"Use your graph to find the half-life of the source. Find it TWICE, starting from two different count rates, and show the lines you drew on the graph. Give the average value.","marks":4,"level":3,"lines":4,"scheme":"Lines shown on graph (1); time for ⟪800\\rightarrow 400\\approx 3.0\\,\\mathrm{min}⟫ (1); time for ⟪400\\rightarrow 200\\approx 6.0-3.0=3.0\\,\\mathrm{min}⟫ (accept 200 → 100: ⟪9.0-6.0=3.0\\,\\mathrm{min}⟫) (1); average half-life ≈ 3.0 min (accept 2.8–3.2 min) (1).","points":[{"text":"Lines shown on graph","marks":1},{"text":"time for ⟪800\\rightarrow 400\\approx 3.0\\,\\mathrm{min}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"time for ⟪400\\rightarrow 200\\approx 6.0-3.0=3.0\\,\\mathrm{min}⟫ (accept 200 → 100: ⟪9.0-6.0=3.0\\,\\mathrm{min}⟫)","marks":1},{"text":"average half-life ≈ 3.0 min (accept 2.8–3.2 min)","marks":1}],"label":"(d)"},{"id":"week12.past.q1.e","kind":"self","q":"Explain why the points do not all lie exactly on a smooth curve.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"Radioactive decay is random (1), so the count in any time interval varies/fluctuates about the expected value (1).","points":[{"text":"Radioactive decay is random","marks":1},{"text":"so the count in any time interval varies/fluctuates about the expected value","marks":1}],"label":"(e)"},{"id":"week12.past.q1.f","kind":"self","q":"Use your value of the half-life to predict the CORRECTED count rate at ⟪t=15\\,\\mathrm{min}⟫, and the count rate the counter would actually show at that time.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"⟪15\\div 3.0=5⟫ half-lives (1); ⟪800\\div 32=25⟫ counts min^−1^ (1); measured ⟪=25+20=45⟫ counts min^−1^ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪15\\div 3.0=5⟫ half-lives","marks":1},{"text":"⟪800\\div 32=25⟫ counts min^−1^","marks":1},{"text":"measured ⟪=25+20=45⟫ counts min^−1^","marks":1}],"label":"(f)"},{"id":"week12.past.q1.g","kind":"self","q":"Another student suggests putting the source in a freezer to make it last longer. Comment on this suggestion.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"It will not work / the half-life does not change (1); radioactive decay is not affected by temperature (physical or chemical conditions) (1).","points":[{"text":"It will not work / the half-life does not change","marks":1},{"text":"radioactive decay is not affected by temperature (physical or chemical conditions)","marks":1}],"label":"(g)"}]},{"id":"week12.past.q2","kind":"multi","title":"Extended response (Paper 02 style)","total":15,"stem":["Many Caribbean countries import most of the oil they use to generate electricity. Nuclear power has been suggested as an alternative."],"stemBlocks":[],"parts":[{"id":"week12.past.q2.a","kind":"self","q":"In a nuclear power station, uranium-235 nuclei undergo fission. Describe what happens in nuclear fission, and explain how a chain reaction is set up.","marks":4,"level":3,"lines":6,"scheme":"A neutron is absorbed by/hits a U-235 nucleus (1); the nucleus splits into two smaller nuclei (1); two or three neutrons are released along with energy (1); these neutrons split more U-235 nuclei, which release more neutrons: a chain reaction (1).","points":[{"text":"A neutron is absorbed by/hits a U-235 nucleus","marks":1},{"text":"the nucleus splits into two smaller nuclei","marks":1},{"text":"two or three neutrons are released along with energy","marks":1},{"text":"these neutrons split more U-235 nuclei, which release more neutrons: a chain reaction","marks":1}],"label":"(a)"},{"id":"week12.past.q2.b","kind":"self","q":"In one fission, the total mass decreases by ⟪3.9\\times 10^{-28}\\,\\mathrm{kg}⟫. Calculate the energy released. ⟪(c=3.0\\times 10^{8}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1})⟫","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"⟪E=mc^{2}⟫ (1); ⟪=3.9\\times 10^{-28}\\times 9.0\\times 10^{16}⟫ (1); ⟪=3.51\\times 10^{-11}\\approx 3.5\\times 10^{-11}\\,\\mathrm{J}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪E=mc^{2}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=3.9\\times 10^{-28}\\times 9.0\\times 10^{16}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=3.51\\times 10^{-11}\\approx 3.5\\times 10^{-11}\\,\\mathrm{J}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":3.5e-11,"sf":2,"unit":"J","unitTex":"\\mathrm{J}"},"label":"(b)","autoKind":"num"},{"id":"week12.past.q2.c","kind":"self","q":"Uranium-238 (^238^~92~U) in the fuel decays by α emission to thorium (Th). The thorium then decays by β emission to protactinium (Pa). Write balanced nuclear equations for BOTH decays.","marks":4,"level":3,"lines":4,"scheme":"⟪{}^{238}_{92}U\\rightarrow {}^{234}_{90}Th+{}^{4}_{2}⟫He (2: products 1, balanced 1); ^234^~90~Th → ^234^~91~Pa + ^0^~−1~e (2: products 1, balanced 1).","points":[{"text":"⟪{}^{238}_{92}U\\rightarrow {}^{234}_{90}Th+{}^{4}_{2}⟫He","marks":2},{"text":"⟪{}^{234}_{90}Th\\rightarrow {}^{234}_{91}\\mathrm{Pa}+{}^{0}_{-1}e⟫","marks":2}],"label":"(c)"},{"id":"week12.past.q2.d","kind":"self","q":"Discuss TWO arguments for and TWO arguments against building a nuclear power station on a Caribbean island.","marks":4,"level":3,"lines":8,"scheme":"For (any two, 1 each): no CO~2~ emissions/helps fight climate change; small mass of fuel gives huge energy; reliable in all weather; less dependence on imported oil. Against (any two, 1 each): long-lived radioactive waste that must be stored safely; risk of accident, increased by hurricanes/earthquakes/volcanoes; very high building and decommissioning cost; fuel must be imported/security risk; little space to store waste or evacuate people.","points":[{"text":"For (any two, 1 each): no CO~2~ emissions/helps fight climate change; small mass of fuel gives huge energy; reliable in all weather; less dependence on imported oil. Against (any two, 1 each): long-lived radioactive waste that must be stored safely; risk of accident, increased by hurricanes/earthquakes/volcanoes; very high building and decommissioning cost; fuel must be imported/security risk; little space to store waste or evacuate people.","marks":4,"each":true}],"label":"(d)"}]}],"n":100}]};
